WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.700
So today, we're going to
talk about architecture,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.080
which, at least to me, has
always been pretty inscrutable.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.680
And so I'm going to take the
approach of just telling you

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.200
everything.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.360
I'm going to go through
all of the modern papers.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.303
And we're going to just look
through what has everyone done.

00:00:22.303 --> 00:00:23.720
And so I've titled
this everything

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.040
you didn't want to know
about architectures

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.840
and hyperparameters,
because I think we all

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.480
wished we lived in a world,
where the only things you had

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.720
to know were like VC
dimension or something, very

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.960
simple theoretical tools, but
that's not really where we are.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.760
OK.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.080
What we are going
to do is, we are

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.040
going to try to understand
architecture from a survey lens.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.900
The best thing to do, better
than listening to this lecture,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.267
even, is for you to go out,
and train your own models,

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:51.600
and try different architectures.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.480
That's, by far, the
best thing to do.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.440
That's part of the
philosophy of the course.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.320
But we're not going to be
able to cover the whole design

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.450
space of all the different
architectures that

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.290
are out there.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.665
That's not something that we
have the compute or the time

00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:04.370
to do.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.730
So my opinion is, the second
best thing that we could do

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.190
is to try to learn from
the experience of others.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.040
What has everyone else done?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.790
What are the choices
that they are making?

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:17.470
And by looking at a broader,
somewhat zoomed out picture,

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.070
maybe we can start
to understand,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.690
oh, these are the kinds
of parameters and choices

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.470
that are fixed across all
effective architectures.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.770
And these other ones can
be varied without impacting

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:28.970
how the model perform.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.130
So I'm going to talk about,
basically, transformer variants,

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.130
like, what is the modern
transformer starting

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.330
with the Vaswani paper?

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.610
And then as we go
to more modern, more

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.903
recent architectures, what
do they have in common?

00:01:42.903 --> 00:01:45.070
And then what are we allowed
to vary or not allowed?

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.790
But what do people vary
as they go through this.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.790
So I think many of you have
taken an NLP course of some kind

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.590
or at least seen a transformer.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.460
So you've probably seen the
very vanilla transformer

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.860
from Vaswani, et al.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.600
There's some fairly standard
choices that you make.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.347
You say, oh, transformers don't
have positional dependence,

00:02:06.347 --> 00:02:08.139
so we're going to add
a position embedding.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.340
And what do we do, we're going
to add some sines and cosines.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.180
We're going to have information
processing through ReLU.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.020
And then we're going
to have a post norm.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.420
I'll talk about what
exactly that is later.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.060
And when you look at
your assignment, your A1,

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.900
you're going to notice some
differences between the standard

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.900
or the vanilla
transformer and what

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.120
we've asked you to implement.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.203
Well, we're going to ask
you to move the LayerNorm

00:02:30.203 --> 00:02:33.700
to the front of each transformer
block or the nonresidual layers.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.647
We're going to ask you to
implement something called RoPE.

00:02:36.647 --> 00:02:38.980
And we're going to ask you
to implement something called

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.660
SwiGLU GeLU and not ReLU.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.340
Why do we pick these?

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.900
One reason is, we've copied a
lot of this over from but Llama,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.220
so did everyone else.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.063
Really, I think, if you were to
train your own language model,

00:02:51.063 --> 00:02:52.980
I think you'll quickly
run into this question,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.740
oh, there's so many choices.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.150
What do I choose for
all of these things?

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.510
And so let's now walk through
all of these different models?

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.710
The way I think
about architectures

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.590
is to look at all the different
things people have done

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.130
and say, what are the things
that people have done?

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.750
Can we pick and
choose from those?

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.930
Percy always makes fun of
me for this a little bit,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.630
but I try to look at all
the different models that

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.592
come out each year to
try to make this lecture.

00:03:19.592 --> 00:03:22.050
And last year, I thought, oh,
there's just a couple papers.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:22.810
It's going to be fine.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:23.570
It's going to be fine.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:24.550
And then I look
through all the things,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.810
and there's a lot of papers.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.880
There's Qwen2, and
Gemma 3, and InternLM2.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.130
And then there were even more.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.750
There's Nemotron-4 and Qwen2.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.770
And oh, my goodness, there
were 19 new dense models.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.870
And so last year, I had
my work cut out for me.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.350
And this year, I thought,
well, there can't

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.010
be that many new LLM releases.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.630
It's got to be slowing down.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.787
People can't keep training
20 dense LLMs per year.

00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:50.370
And that's technically right,
there aren't as many dense LLMs.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.630
Initially, I was like,
oh, there's Qwen3.

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.460
Gemma 4 just came
out last Thursday,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.160
so I put that in
there, and Olmo 3.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.572
There's only a couple.

00:03:57.572 --> 00:03:59.280
And of course, I have
to give a shout out

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.700
to Percy's own AP model
trained with Marin.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.200
And I was like, oh, we'll just
have a few things to cover.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.825
But it turns out, if
you start looking,

00:04:05.825 --> 00:04:07.880
there's a lot of
different models.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.140
And so the fact that we have
so many different models,

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.780
most of these actually are
MoEs, mixtures of experts.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.320
And I'll be talking about that
tomorrow rather than today.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.459
Because we have such a
big diversity of models,

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:21.279
we actually get a
pretty good picture

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:24.160
of all the different
choices that we can make.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:25.780
So I made this little table.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.440
We'll come back to this little
table at the end of the lecture.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.360
But basically, at
this point, starting

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.800
with the original
transformer, there's

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.760
been actually quite a few
autoregressive language

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.440
models trained on the
same class of things.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.280
And you can ask
questions like, what are

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.040
the different vocabulary sizes?

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.440
Or what kind of layer
norms do we use?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.520
Or what kind of position
embeddings do people use?

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.020
And we see some
fairly clear trends.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.330
I'll be talking
about this as we go.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:58.982
So the goal here is that
we're going to cover

00:04:58.982 --> 00:05:00.190
a couple of different things.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.790
We're going to cover common
architecture variations.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.920
So these are different building
blocks of the transformer.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.170
And after we've established
what the standard building

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.810
blocks are, like, what do we
use for the nonlinearities?

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.912
Or what do we use for
position embeddings?

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:15.870
Then we're going to talk
about hyperparameters.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.537
We're going to go
down even lower detail

00:05:17.537 --> 00:05:20.330
and say, what is ff_dim?

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.010
Should we make that
a multiple of four

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.410
or multiply the hidden
by four to get ff_dim?

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:28.223
How many vocab
elements should I have?

00:05:28.223 --> 00:05:29.890
And then after that,
we're going to talk

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.770
about very low-level
tricks of how

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.705
to get models to train stably.

00:05:34.705 --> 00:05:37.330
And the reason why I'm going to
talk about that in this lecture

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.250
is because these
stability tricks

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.770
have a pretty close connection
with the architecture variation.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.210
One of the things that higher
level I want to impress upon you

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.670
is that architectures are
actually a very complex set

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:51.710
of trade offs.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.870
What does the
architecture have to do?

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.690
Well, it has to learn from
data, so it has to generalize.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.950
It has to train
efficiently on GPUs.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:00.570
And it has to not blow up.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:04.110
Halfway through training,
if your training loss

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.650
is just go down like this
and then suddenly blow up,

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:07.870
that's no good at all.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:09.790
So all these
different requirements

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.957
end up getting baked straight
into the architecture.

00:06:11.957 --> 00:06:13.790
And that's why these
things are a little bit

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.155
messy and a little bit complex.

00:06:16.155 --> 00:06:17.530
But you should
keep that in mind.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:21.030
And that's why things are,
in many ways, not so elegant.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.950
So we're going to start with
the core architecture piece.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:29.270
And as a high-level
view, I think the way

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:31.270
that I see a lot of
the architecture stuff,

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.390
looking, basically,
historically,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:38.590
is in the early days of starting
with a transformer until GPT-3

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:39.150
or so.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.090
There's a lot of
experimentation that happens.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:42.890
People try lots of
different things.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.830
There's no gold standard
that everyone has unified on.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.710
And then Llama 2 comes out.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:49.540
And everyone's like,
wow, Llama 2 is great,

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.200
I want my own Llama 2.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.800
And so everyone starts
training Llama 2 alikes

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.720
with minor variation
that people have.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.520
And then finally, last year,
we saw really big differences

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.080
or a trend towards
architecture modifications

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.800
that make training more stable.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.320
And this year, we
see lots of trends

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.000
towards architecture variations
that enable longer context

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:13.960
dependence.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:16.140
So there are these big
themes that are happening.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.595
But really, I think
you see this big point

00:07:18.595 --> 00:07:20.720
when Llama 2 comes out,
and everyone's like, wow, I

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:21.845
want to train some of that.

00:07:21.845 --> 00:07:24.220
And then suddenly, or not
suddenly, but after that,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:25.970
people are starting
to explore once again.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.640
So it's cool to see all of
these different changes.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.080
OK.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.920
I think people can disagree
about a lot of things

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.360
on architectures, but
there is one thing

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.740
that everyone agrees on.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.280
If you take the transformer
paper, I think a lot of people

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.600
say like, the transformer
people got most of the things,

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:45.920
except this.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:47.930
And the thing that they
really did not get right

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:51.050
or I think, most people
agree, they did not get right

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.250
is where you put the layer norm.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.690
So in the original
transformer paper,

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:01.090
the layer norm goes in what you
would call the residual path.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.570
So in the transformer, you
have the residual stream

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.210
that's x, that runs
through the whole network.

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:08.950
And then you are going
to be applying attention,

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.590
which does some computation.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:15.530
And it will add back a delta
back into the residual stream.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:18.450
And then, in order to make
sure that these gradients are

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.410
stable across
layers, a layer norm

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:24.970
is placed at the end of
each of these components.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.410
Now, instead of
putting the layer norms

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.170
in the residual stream,
there's an alternative,

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:35.650
I'll refer to this as prenorm,
in which you can put the layer

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:38.150
norm outside of the
residual stream,

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.747
but before each of
the computations.

00:08:39.747 --> 00:08:41.789
So you can put it before
the multihead attention,

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.420
you can put it before the FFN.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.340
We'll call this prenorm.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.340
The nomenclature will get
a little bit confusing.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.600
You can call this
postnorm for now,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.540
but let's call
this residual norm,

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.740
because you're putting the
norm in the residual layer.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.220
Basically, all modern
language models

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.120
push the layer norm outside
of the residual stream.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.340
This is just like a thing that
basically, everybody does.

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.460
There is one funny
exception, but it is OPT350M.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:14.700
And if you all are familiar
with language models, we know,

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.380
OPT, in general, was a
mess of a language model.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.020
And OPT 350M is even more so
because I don't why only that

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:28.940
model has a postlayer norm
in the residual stream.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.620
OK.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.500
So this is one of the things
that everyone agrees on.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:39.220
And so you might wonder, why
is this such a unified thing

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.950
across all of the
different models?

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:44.950
And if you look at
some of the early work,

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.550
studying, where do you place the
layer norm style research, what

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:54.110
you really see is that the early
motivation for a lot of this

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:56.690
was, when you train
a transformer,

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:57.810
you need to do a warm up.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:09:59.710
Actually, modern
transformer training

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:01.390
still does warm up as well.

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.590
But you definitely need to
do warm up when you train.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.770
Now, wouldn't it be nice if
we could remove the warm up.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:09.470
So that was the
initial motivation

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:11.310
for a lot of this research.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.150
But people quickly realized
that removing the warm up

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:18.350
had very serious issues in terms
of the stability and convergence

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:19.170
of these things.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.910
So if you did postnorm plus
layer norm, which is basically

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:25.930
the original transformer thing,
you got this purple dashed line,

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:30.190
oh, you just don't converge as
well compared to doing something

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:33.050
like prenorm, you can
ignore the other terms,

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.750
you would get much
nicer convergence

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:35.890
even without warm up.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.710
So this was the
original motivation.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:41.640
But really, what
people quickly realized

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:46.000
is that moving the layer norms
outside the residual stream

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.380
has some pretty
important implications

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.840
as you make your network deeper
and as you start to grapple

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.440
with stability issues.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.440
To me, I think the
gradient attenuation

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:00.280
issues are the most clear.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.380
When you talk to people
who do architecture design,

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.780
I'm not really one of the people
that deeply engages in this.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:08.600
But one of the things
that people often say

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.840
is, keep your
residual stream clean.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:12.600
So in this case,
you have your x's

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.000
coming in from the
on, the prenorm side.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.800
And this x propagates all
the way up to the top,

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.140
all the way up to
your final output.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.360
And that allows gradients to
propagate in the backward pass,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.540
straight through this.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:28.680
That makes gradient
propagation very simple,

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:32.380
which improves both stability
and signal propagation.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.080
And that's what people
realized very, very quickly,

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.960
that if you do
something like prenorm

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:43.470
in blue at initialization, the
gradient sizes remains the same,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.930
because you have this nice,
straight through propagation

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.290
in the backward pass.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:49.830
On the other hand, if
you have post LayerNorm,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.810
you have these
complicated effects

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:55.002
that happen, because your
LayerNorming, each time,

00:11:55.002 --> 00:11:56.710
you're going through
a transformer block.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:58.918
And that's going to change
the norm of your gradients

00:11:58.918 --> 00:12:00.930
as you go backwards through.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:04.690
So you can see,
from the principle

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.990
of keep your residual
stream clean,

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.450
that prenorm makes
a lot of sense.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.810
People also realized
through experimentation

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.430
that this also improves
stability, in general,

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:18.730
that the sizes and
frequencies of gradient spikes

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.910
were improved under prenorm
compared to postnorm.

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.475
And this is a figure
from Salazar and Ngyuen,

00:12:24.475 --> 00:12:25.850
who were one of
the first ones, I

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.890
think, to study this
phenomena carefully.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:30.950
I think this is the reason
why it's stuck around.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:33.730
Stability and the
ability to go deep

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:37.260
are both very, very important
for modern large language

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:37.920
models.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.500
And so this idea of moving
your LayerNorm outside

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:44.020
of the residual stream is
one that basically everyone

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.460
has adopted.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:52.380
So now, if putting LayerNorms
in residual streams is bad,

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.720
why does LayerNorm have
to be at the start?

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.640
Of course, we have prenorm,
which is before our computation.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.720
But we could have it
after computation as well.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:03.660
That's equally good, at
least under that logic.

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.660
And that's exactly right.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:10.220
Many recent models, like
Grok, or Gemma 2, or OLMo 2,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.500
have this structure where
they've moved the LayerNorm

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:13.860
after the computation.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.540
So it's a postnorm
of a kind, but it's

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.422
outside the residual stream.

00:13:17.422 --> 00:13:19.880
Other models still actually
just put LayerNorms everywhere.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.000
They put a LayerNorm here.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.260
They put a LayerNorm after.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.940
I'll get to this later as
we talk about stability.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:27.860
But one of the
other lessons that

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.820
seems to have held
up very well is,

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.740
if you have stability issues,
you can sprinkle in LayerNorms

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:33.620
everywhere.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:36.238
And that will generally
improve stability.

00:13:36.238 --> 00:13:38.030
It's almost very strange
to be saying this,

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:39.170
because it's so ridiculous.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:41.610
And yet, that statement has
actually been proven right.

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:44.445
Every time people have
encountered stability issues,

00:13:44.445 --> 00:13:46.070
they say, oh, but
what if we just throw

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:47.630
a LayerNorm into attention?

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:48.850
Turns out, that works too.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:49.850
We'll get to that later.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.030
OK, that's postnorm
or double norm,

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:55.230
in this case, where you
have two LayerNorms here.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:56.190
OK.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.790
The other thing that you can do
is, in the original transformer,

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.570
you have the layer norm, which
is this operation right here.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.830
So you have your activations x,
you're going to mean subtract,

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.090
divide the variance, and
then scale it back up.

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:10.090
And this works just fine.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:11.670
It's not like this is wrong.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.230
And many models
have successfully

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:14.990
trained on this scheme.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:19.670
But basically, most or all
modern models, I think,

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:22.310
use RMSNorm, which
doesn't subtract the mean

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:23.610
or add a bias term.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:26.770
So it's just a scaling
down and scaling back up.

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:29.750
So you can see this
in the equation here.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.210
And really, LayerNorm is
more expressive than RMSNorm.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:35.040
So there's really
representationally

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:38.440
no reason why you
have to use RMSNorm.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:42.680
But RMSNorm is nice,
because in practice, there's

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.440
really no expressiveness loss.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.400
RMSNorm models just
as well as LayerNorm.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:50.640
But more importantly,
it is faster.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.040
This is the part where the
systems and architecture

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.240
codesign starts to come in.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.920
Firstly mentioned in the
previous lecture, this idea

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.360
of arithmetic intensity.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.160
We want to keep our
GPUs hot by doing

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:06.360
matrix multiplies and other
very intense computations.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.200
We do not want to
be wasting our GPUs

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:11.660
by having them move little tiny
bits of memory back and forth.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:16.160
That's a very inefficient
use of our very powerful GPU.

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:20.000
And so what we want is to
remove operations that are small

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.840
and involve memory
movement, but don't give us

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.060
much expressive power.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.560
So by that view, what we
really want to be doing here

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:30.840
is, if the mean subtraction
and addition isn't really

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.000
doing much for us.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.300
Just get rid of it.

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:37.200
You might think, OK,
why does this matter?

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.100
We're just optimizing
this teeny tiny operation

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.020
that accounts for, in
this case, something

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:49.300
like 0.17% of the total floating
point operations of our system.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:53.500
But as Percy mentioned, it's
not really about the flops.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:56.060
The flops are the floating
point operations we do,

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:59.340
that's multiplying matrices,
but that's not runtime.

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.700
Runtime is a much more
complicated object.

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:06.680
And statistical normalizations,
things like LayerNorm,

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.400
even though they're
only 0.17% of the flops,

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:11.840
depending on your workload
and depending on the setup,

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.980
can be up to 25% of the runtime.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.120
That's crazy.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.460
On tiny models, this can
be really, really big,

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:20.100
because you're still having to
move all these parameters back

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:23.460
and forth from fast to slow
memory and vice versa when

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:24.880
you're doing these operations.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.700
So data movement is
really, really important.

00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:31.110
And RMSNorm can still matter
a lot because of this.

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:35.070
So you can see the
difference here.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.370
The arithmetic
intensity is in white.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:40.410
And then you can see the
flops involved in black.

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.488
And you see that LayerNorm has
a very low arithmetic intensity,

00:16:43.488 --> 00:16:45.030
which is the operation
we try to want

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.530
to remove as much as possible.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.670
Yeah, question over there?

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.830
The movement for normalization
is so disproportionate

00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:54.750
compared to density [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.510
So for something like
tensor contraction, which

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:59.990
is, in this case,
matrix multiplies,

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:03.350
the majority of the
workload is multiplying,

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:06.150
whereas for stat normalization,
the majority of the workload

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:07.210
is memory movement.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:08.650
And memory movement
is quite slow.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:11.910
So imagine the case, where
moving something is almost

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:12.770
all the compute.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.270
Then you're still
paying quite a bit

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:18.192
here, because activations
can be quite large.

00:17:18.192 --> 00:17:20.109
Yeah, I think the percent
runtime in this case

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:21.210
is quite extreme.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:24.710
This is like in the tiny models
with matrices that don't really

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.490
generally make sense
in modern workloads,

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.760
but this is giving
you a sense of why

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:31.720
this is a free optimization.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:32.760
And you do see this.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.600
This is another
paper in which people

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:37.100
were evaluating different
architecture interventions,

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:38.920
Narang et al in 2020.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.300
I think this was a Google paper.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:42.160
And they show, for
teeny tiny transformer

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:45.220
of a 200 million parameters,
you get more steps per second.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:48.080
That's the third column over
here when you switch to RMSNorm.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.372
And in fact, you actually
get better performance, which

00:17:50.372 --> 00:17:52.560
I don't think is something
that you're guaranteed,

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:54.260
but it's a nice
bonus, regardless.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:58.060
So you get a free systems win
by just moving to RMSNorm.

00:17:58.060 --> 00:17:59.840
And so basically,
everyone has decided

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.720
to move over to this now.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.492
And in general, there's a
more general version of this.

00:18:06.492 --> 00:18:08.700
There's no guarantee to any
of the things I'm saying.

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:12.880
But biased terms in
transformers and neural networks

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:14.900
are generally not that useful.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.200
So in the original
transformer, the linear terms

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:21.160
all have biases, but most
implementations actually

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:23.040
just drop the biases entirely.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.400
Once again, this is another
example of something

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:28.930
that's not very arithmetically
intense, but fairly memory

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.050
intensive, relatively speaking.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:33.210
And so you might as
well just drop these

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.490
and get the free systems win.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:36.970
There's also some
cases, I'll just

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:39.290
mention this offhand, where
the bias terms can also

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:42.637
induce stability issues, so
they're useful in other ways.

00:18:42.637 --> 00:18:44.970
But really, I think the primary
reason these are dropped

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:49.450
is just to simplify things
from a systems perspective.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:51.770
Cool.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:52.490
OK.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.610
So I think layer norms,
the story is pretty easy.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:57.890
It's easy in the sense
that what people do

00:18:57.890 --> 00:18:59.470
is fairly standardized.

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.670
Our understanding, not like
a deep theoretical level,

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:07.450
but our understanding of what
layer norm does is fairly good.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:11.010
Everyone moves the layer norm
outside the residual stream,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.030
often prenorm.

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:16.210
But I think this might partially
be because Llama 2 did that.

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:18.090
And we roughly
have a sense of how

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:22.850
to use LayerNorm to control
things like gradient spikes

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:25.580
and keep signal
propagation nice.

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:29.255
Related to that, we also now
basically always use RMSNorm.

00:19:29.255 --> 00:19:31.380
And you hopefully understand
the general principles

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:34.920
here of basically, just
dropping bias terms.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.180
And that allows us to keep
our system more arithmetically

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:43.060
intense while keeping the
expressive power the same.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.980
I think the unsatisfying thing
about a lot of architectures

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:48.780
is that, you can't really
reason about this beforehand,

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:52.420
but we don't beforehand that
dropping the bias terms is OK.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:54.220
But from a lot of
experimentation and now,

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:55.740
collectively acquired
knowledge, we

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:59.020
roughly that dropping the
bias terms on both the linear

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:02.838
and RMSNorm is OK for typical
language modeling workloads.

00:20:02.838 --> 00:20:04.380
This is the kind of
statement that we

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:06.420
can make on the
basis of what we do

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:09.620
when we look at a variety
of different models.

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:13.900
Any questions for
LayerNorm stuff?

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:14.580
Good.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:15.260
OK.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.220
So now, I'm going to
talk about activations.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:20.320
And there's a whole
zoo of activations.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:21.420
There's just a lot--

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:27.350
ReLU, GeLU, Swish, ELU,
GeGLU, SeLU, SwiGLU, LiGLU.

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.470
And what are these things?

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:33.830
I think, at one point of
my more stats ML training,

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:36.090
I thought to myself, I will
never learn these things.

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.910
I will make it a point of pride
to never know what a SwiGLU is.

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:40.590
But now, it's actually
very important

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:43.310
for us to actually have
a general sense of what

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:46.470
these objects are and
which parts of these names

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:51.030
actually matter for performance.

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:55.430
So you can build and train
a language model on just

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:59.350
a fairly vanilla activation.

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:01.550
I guess, Chinchilla is
probably the best model

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:02.370
out of that group.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.270
But even if you
just want a ReLU,

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:06.910
you can train a reasonably
performant language model

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:08.290
using just that activation.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:09.990
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.050
And if we move to GeLU, which
is a Gaussian error unit,

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:15.950
and really, the only difference
is this tiny divot at the bottom

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:19.502
here, which really, for
the most of the activation

00:21:19.502 --> 00:21:20.960
doesn't change
anything but changes

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.200
the gradients right near zero.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:25.440
Then you can train
models like GPT 3.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:27.200
That's a perfectly
good large language

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:32.040
model, not modern by modern
standards, but perfectly fine.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:34.720
But then we get to the
gated linear units,

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:36.240
like SwiGLU and GeGLU.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.080
And these are really where
most of the action is.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:41.920
This is very similar
to LayerNorm in that,

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.520
I think almost all
credible modern language

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:49.880
models use a gated
linear unit of some kind.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:50.460
OK.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.480
So what is gated linear unit?

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:54.303
So these are gated activations.

00:21:54.303 --> 00:21:55.720
So if we want to
look at something

00:21:55.720 --> 00:21:58.180
like a feed forward
layer, we can just

00:21:58.180 --> 00:21:59.180
look at this first part.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:02.020
This is a very standard
ReLU feed forward.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:02.840
I have my x.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:05.040
I hit it with a W1.

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:07.000
I entrywise threshold at 0.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:08.620
And then I hit it
with another W2.

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:09.620
I get my output right.

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:11.580
Very straightforward
ReLU network.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:16.860
I don't say this as my
personal experience,

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.730
but another thing that is often
said in architecture design

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.990
is that gating is
often very helpful.

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:24.490
So if you apply that very
general heuristic, what

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:27.170
you might get is to
say, OK, well, instead

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:30.370
of just having this
entrywise ReLU,

00:22:30.370 --> 00:22:32.050
why don't we also have a gate?

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:34.130
And the second gate,
the second term

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:38.090
here, this is just going to
multiply the output of my ReLU,

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:39.070
entrywise.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:42.010
And I have a second
matrix V. OK.

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:45.890
Now, this is just going to
modulate the output of my ReLU.

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.510
And then I'm going to do
everything else the same.

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:53.710
So instead of just having
xW1, W2, I have xW1,

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:55.630
and I'm going to
gate that with xV.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:59.070
This is another activation
the same size as this.

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:02.970
And then I'm going to down
project it back with W2.

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:03.470
OK.

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:04.330
So what is this?

00:23:04.330 --> 00:23:05.730
Now, this is a ReGLU.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.850
You make these names by
adding the first activation,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:14.010
in this case, ReLU and GLU,
so the ReLU gated linear unit.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:17.900
And gating has been a very
effective other primitive

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:19.280
in architecture design.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.980
And it turns out that this
is very effective in language

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:23.040
modeling as well.

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:26.787
So if you take
something like a GeLU,

00:23:26.787 --> 00:23:28.120
we've already talked about that.

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:30.420
That's the ReLU, but with a
little divot at the bottom

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:33.780
here, you will get a GeGLU.

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:37.220
And if you take a SwiGLU,
which is x times a sigmoid,

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:39.440
then you will get a SwiGLU.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:42.740
So this is a squish
times the rest of it.

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:47.020
And this really covers a
lot of the modern models.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:49.760
Generally, the Google
folks have used GeGLU,

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:53.460
so like the Gemma models,
the T5 models are those.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:57.340
And everything that's a Llama
descendant uses a SwiGLU.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.980
So PaLM and the
Llama descendants

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:03.140
are all SwiGLU models.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:06.280
I would say that SwiGLU is
probably the more dominant one,

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:10.420
but honestly, amongst the gated
units, doesn't really matter.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:14.460
Now, here's a side note that
will be a semi-important piece

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:16.270
of trivia later.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:19.670
If you look up here,
you will notice

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:23.430
that there are more parameters
for the gated model, because I

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:27.050
have this parameter V. And so
if you do a little bit of math,

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:31.390
I now have three matrices
instead of two matrices.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:34.870
What you should do is, you
should maybe use a smaller

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:37.910
feedforward dimension
by a factor of 2/3,

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:40.490
in order to keep the
parameter count the same.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.890
So this is roughly
the idea of, well,

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:45.150
I want to keep the same
number of total parameters

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:48.412
as my original MLP, but I
now want to make it gated.

00:24:48.412 --> 00:24:50.370
So I'm going to make the
feedforward dimension,

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:52.630
which is the output
dimension of this W,

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.830
a little bit smaller by 2/3.

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:57.530
So this is a general rule of
thumb that people have followed,

00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:01.790
but it's not really
an iron rule.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:04.510
The original Noam
Shazeer paper that

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:10.830
proposed this had some very
small deltas originally,

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:12.930
but they're consistent deltas.

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:16.730
And I think to his credit,
I think a lot of his papers

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:20.170
have these error bar assessments
of training multiple replicates

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:22.570
and checking to see
if they're better.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.890
And if you look,
the GLU variants

00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:29.278
are almost always consistently
better than the nonGLU variants.

00:25:29.278 --> 00:25:31.070
And this is a parameter
matched comparison,

00:25:31.070 --> 00:25:35.170
because Noam Shazeer is always
doing this 2/3 adjustment

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:37.970
to make sure that all of the
models have the same total

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:40.810
number of parameters.

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:42.270
So this is quite nice.

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:44.410
It's, in some ways, a free win.

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:46.870
Almost everyone uses a GLU.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:49.570
There have been
other more controlled

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:50.670
systematic comparisons.

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:52.850
This is the same paper I
was talking about before,

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:55.090
Narang et al in 2020.

00:25:55.090 --> 00:26:00.010
Google, actually, in the 2020s,
did quite a few nice large scale

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:03.690
architecture comparison papers,
although with a T5 architecture

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:07.330
and not an autoregressive
language model.

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:09.690
And they basically
comprehensively compare things

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:10.390
like GLU.

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:12.020
And you see, once
again, if we look

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:15.880
at the SwiGLU, or the GeGLU,
or the GLUs in general,

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:17.620
they do significantly
better at loss

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:19.200
or the other downstream metrics.

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:24.420
Fairly compelling on these
papers, also clear from now

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:30.340
a lot of model training runs
that SwiGLU and GLU are good.

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:32.400
So there's a lot of
variations in gating,

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:34.700
but really, the important
single axis to know

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:38.500
is that gating for these
nonlinearities is actually

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:41.940
quite important, gives you
a nice boost without much

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:44.500
of a computational cost.

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:47.400
That's not to say that gated
linear units are necessary.

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:49.580
I mean, GPT 3 was that--

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:52.960
I think, the Nemotron 340B
model used a Squared ReLU,

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:56.380
which is a crazy choice,
but that works too.

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:58.720
Both of these models are
perfectly performant,

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:01.580
but it's actually quite rare
to see anything that's not

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:03.780
trained on a gated linear unit.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:06.180
So evidence is pointing
towards consistent gains

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:09.510
on using these gating tricks.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:14.750
So those are, I think, the more
consensus choices for things

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:16.870
that we can do in architecture.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:19.130
Now, this one, I think,
is a really fun idea,

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:22.470
but one that, I think, now, the
test of time has shown maybe

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:26.390
is not quite as good or maybe
not as popular of an idea.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:29.550
Normally, we do our
transformer blocks serially.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:31.870
We compute our attention,
then we compute

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:34.710
the MLP, one after the other.

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.230
If you're very
systems-minded, you

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:38.670
might say, well, this
introduces a bottleneck.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:40.990
I have to wait for
the computation of one

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:42.130
to do the other.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:43.690
If they were,
instead, in parallel,

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:46.030
I could bring to bear
some new and cool systems

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:47.990
optimizations, potentially.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.310
So you might ask, could we
parallelize the transformer

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:51.830
block?

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:55.190
And this was
originally an idea that

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:58.550
was in GPTJ, which is
an open source attempted

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:01.830
replication of GPT 3.

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:04.870
And very interestingly,
I think GPTJ

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:09.200
has been surprisingly
influential in propagating

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:10.180
a lot of ideas.

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:11.300
I mean, PaLM as well.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:14.960
Google is actually surprisingly
bold with the architectures

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:16.040
that they do.

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:18.880
But the description
in PaLM, which

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:21.060
you can see in their
report, is the following--

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:22.600
instead of nesting
this, which is

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:24.560
the sequential firm
at the top, you're

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:26.520
just going to add
together the output

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:28.320
of the MLP and the
attention layer

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:32.840
and just add both of those
back into the residual stream.

00:28:32.840 --> 00:28:34.970
If you implement this
right, you can actually

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:36.220
share a lot of the components.

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:37.660
You can share the LayerNorms.

00:28:37.660 --> 00:28:40.140
You can fuse the
matrix multiplies.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.760
This allows you to potentially
get additional systems

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.040
optimizations.

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:46.760
And I think a lot
of the people that

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:48.820
have been influenced by Google.

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:50.680
So Cohere, I know,
was founded from one

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:53.400
of the former
transformer authors.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.620
They do a lot of
Google-inspired optimizations.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:59.860
They followed this architecture,
but not very many others.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.120
This has been an approach
that has really fallen out

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:04.880
of popularity over the
past, I think, two years,

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:08.090
I think mainly because
optimization of the serial form

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.290
has gotten sufficiently
good that the system's

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:12.210
gains from the
second one just isn't

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:15.770
worth the small hits
to representation power

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:19.670
that you end up getting,
going from parallel to serial.

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:21.170
Effectively, you
can think about it,

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:23.130
as you've lost
half of your depth.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:29.290
And that can be a deleterious
thing to do to your model.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:33.570
So in terms of the
architecture things, actually,

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:36.970
the fact that this is so
short should suggest to you

00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:40.472
how much the original
transformer formulation has

00:29:40.472 --> 00:29:42.930
somewhat stood the test of
time, because the only thing I'm

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:47.610
really talking about changing
here is where the norms go,

00:29:47.610 --> 00:29:51.790
or whether we have bias terms,
or whether we get the MLPs.

00:29:51.790 --> 00:29:54.010
But those are actually
pretty minor changes

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:56.530
compared to all the
things that you can do.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:59.850
Now, those of you that are
carefully paying attention

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:03.050
might say, but wait, there's a
lot of transformer alternatives

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:04.940
that change the attention.

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:08.000
Yes, you'll have to wait until
next lecture, because today,

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:12.140
I'm just only going to cover
core attention-based methods.

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:14.820
And next lecture, I'll throw
in a little bit of state space

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:15.640
model stuff.

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:18.973
But as long as you're in this
dense attention land, actually,

00:30:18.973 --> 00:30:21.140
the architecture from the
original transformer paper

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:23.940
is pretty close to what we do.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:25.860
So you see quite a bit of this.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:29.380
So just now going back to
this, blue here is RMSNorm,

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:30.680
black is LayerNorm.

00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:32.300
You see most of
the modern models

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:35.485
are RMSNorm models, serial
versus parallel layers.

00:30:35.485 --> 00:30:37.360
The blue one is parallel,
the rest is serial.

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:41.080
You see mostly serial layers,
prenorm versus postnorm.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.580
Some of these ones that
I marked as postnorm

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:46.180
are actually pre and postnorm.

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:48.180
And then these ones
on the right, these

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:51.140
are GLUs, almost always,
with the exception

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:54.320
of things like Falcon, which
use a gated linear unit.

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:56.060
But almost all of
these are really

00:30:56.060 --> 00:30:59.420
gated linear units
for modern models.

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:02.470
So you can see the
trends quite visually

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:04.190
from what I'm telling you.

00:31:04.190 --> 00:31:04.870
OK.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:07.550
So really, the
thing that is very

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:09.375
different across
implementations,

00:31:09.375 --> 00:31:11.750
and I think a place where a
lot of the architecture stuff

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:16.190
is still in flux is how
you do position dependence

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:18.610
and incorporate information
from other positions.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:22.550
So the core attention
component, in some sense.

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:24.670
So there are lots
of different ways

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:27.990
that you can encode
position into a transformer.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:30.350
And just to remind
you right, this

00:31:30.350 --> 00:31:32.510
is very, very important,
because attention

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:33.850
is positionally independent.

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:36.450
They're just inner products,
so you can just shuffle them.

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:38.950
And attention would be the same
if you don't have a position

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:39.990
embedding.

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.910
The original transformer had
sine and cosine embeddings,

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:44.870
a Fourier transform
intuition that if you

00:31:44.870 --> 00:31:47.830
have sines and cosines, then
you can recover position

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:50.030
from that, no matter what.

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:54.070
A number of other large models
that followed soon after that

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:57.470
used absolute embeddings,
where each position had

00:31:57.470 --> 00:32:00.190
its own different embedding.

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:03.200
And then several
other Google models

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.100
like to use relative embeddings.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:10.080
And here, you're not adding
embeddings into the word vector

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:13.240
embeddings, but instead, you're
adding a vector to the attention

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:14.440
computation itself.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:16.320
So if you're three
positions off,

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:19.580
the attention matrix gets a
different offset added to it.

00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:24.880
And models like T5 and
Chinchilla use this scheme.

00:32:24.880 --> 00:32:27.720
The thing that has really
become pretty dominant

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:30.680
in terms of position
embedding is this class

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:32.760
of embeddings called
RoPE, which some of you

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:34.440
may be familiar with.

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:37.540
Most models past 2024 use
this type of embedding.

00:32:37.540 --> 00:32:40.760
And it's remarkable, given
that RoPE, in some ways,

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:42.720
came out of nowhere.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:47.360
Originally, I think this was
also a GPTJ innovation from,

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:53.080
I think, not very well-known
blog post and paper combination

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.440
from an author in China.

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:58.200
But really, it has some
really interesting ideas

00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:01.330
for why you would do
something like RoPE.

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:04.530
So RoPE is a relative
position embedding.

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:06.190
And a relative
position embedding,

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:10.170
let's make an opinionated
stance that I should not

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:13.590
care about the absolute
position of any words.

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:17.190
So if you A and apple
appear together,

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.930
even if it appears at
the start or at the end,

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:28.410
in RoPE embeddings, they
should get the same result.

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:30.590
And we want to represent
it in this way.

00:33:30.590 --> 00:33:34.430
So I have an embedding f, and
I have another embedding f.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:36.850
And these are going to take
in the identity of the words x

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:40.290
and y and the positions
absolute of i and j.

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:41.772
And I want this to be equal.

00:33:41.772 --> 00:33:43.730
If I take the inner
product of these embeddings

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:47.170
to be equal to a
function, that only

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:50.530
depends on the
relative difference.

00:33:50.530 --> 00:33:52.450
And every existing
embedding before it

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:54.370
didn't really fulfill
this equality.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:58.140
Sine is not relative, because it
has these absolute cross terms

00:33:58.140 --> 00:33:59.380
that are not relative.

00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:01.940
Absolute position embeddings,
just by the name of it,

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:03.820
is obviously not relative.

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:07.360
And then relative embeddings,
technically, these are relative,

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.699
but they're not embeddings,
because they're just

00:34:09.699 --> 00:34:11.159
adding to the attention matrix.

00:34:11.159 --> 00:34:13.340
So there's no inner
product structure

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:16.100
that you can extract
out of this guy.

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:19.020
So given this, you
might ask, is there

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:23.699
a nice way that we can truly
have this relative embedding?

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:25.420
And the idea is very cool.

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:28.580
It's really just looking
at other properties

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:30.900
about angles and cosines.

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:34.284
So we want our embeddings to be
invariant to absolute positions.

00:34:34.284 --> 00:34:36.076
And we know that inner
products of any kind

00:34:36.076 --> 00:34:39.000
are invariant to
arbitrary rotation.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:44.020
So the idea is to say, I'm
going to take my semantic word

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:47.000
vectors, the ones that are
independent of any position.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:48.540
So this is my starting point.

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:51.219
And then I'm going to rotate
each of these vectors,

00:34:51.219 --> 00:34:55.880
in this case, in 2D, based on
the position that the words

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:56.639
appear.

00:34:56.639 --> 00:35:03.220
So just as a simple example,
let's say we have the sentence,

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:04.740
"We know that."

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:07.640
"We" appears as at position 0,
so I'm not going to touch that

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:08.140
at all.

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.080
I'm just going to
keep that where it is.

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:13.560
The word "no" is at position
1, so I'm going to rotate it

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:14.580
by some angle.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:18.000
And that's my one
position rotation.

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:20.920
Now, what happens if
I apply the same idea

00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:23.460
to the following sequence?

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:24.660
Of course, we know.

00:35:24.660 --> 00:35:26.920
In this case, "we" and
"no" are still adjacent.

00:35:26.920 --> 00:35:28.420
They're right next
to each other,

00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:30.180
but their absolute
position has shifted.

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:32.920
"Of course" comes
before "we know now."

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:35.480
In this case, I'm going
to rotate the word "we"

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:39.440
by two positions, because
it's two index, 0, 1, 2.

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:42.420
So the word "we" is in the
second position number 2,

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:43.880
so I rotate by two.

00:35:43.880 --> 00:35:45.800
I rotate "no" by
three positions,

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:47.560
because it's in
position number 3.

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:50.440
And what do you know the
relative angle between these two

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:51.940
is still separated by 1.

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:56.930
So this is a very, very simple
idea of just using rotations

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.950
to represent position.

00:35:59.950 --> 00:36:02.590
And if we do that, then anytime
we take an inner product,

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:04.770
those inner products are
going to be invariant

00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:06.730
of absolute positions.

00:36:06.730 --> 00:36:09.298
Now, you might say, well,
in two dimensions, that's

00:36:09.298 --> 00:36:11.590
pretty easy, because you've
only really got one choice,

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:13.790
you've got clockwise
and counterclockwise.

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:17.090
But in high dimensions, there's
an infinite space of ways

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:18.350
that you can rotate vectors.

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:20.650
So what do you do
in D dimensions?

00:36:20.650 --> 00:36:23.070
Well, you do the simplest
possible thing, and it works.

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:26.370
The simplest possible thing
is to reduce it to the 3D case

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:27.150
repeatedly.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:28.710
So you have a D
dimensional vector.

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:31.210
Just cut it up
into chunks of two.

00:36:31.210 --> 00:36:35.230
And each pair of two
dimensions gets rotated.

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:38.450
And the theta at which
these things rotate vary.

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:40.070
Some of them are
very low frequency,

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:42.410
so they rotate very
slowly, so they can

00:36:42.410 --> 00:36:44.350
capture long range dependence.

00:36:44.350 --> 00:36:45.790
Some of them rotate
very quickly,

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:48.373
so they capture things like, are
they neighbors to each other?

00:36:48.373 --> 00:36:53.400
And then at the end, after I've
rotated every pair of vectors,

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:55.360
I get my final embedding.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:58.860
So this is the RoPE approach.

00:36:58.860 --> 00:37:02.860
The paper, if you read it,
has a very complex motivation

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:05.100
about complex
numbers, but really, I

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:07.720
think the intuitive way, at
least to me to think about it,

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:10.172
is to just, you want
to rotate by reducing

00:37:10.172 --> 00:37:12.380
to the two-dimensional case,
and you're just rotating

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:15.660
every pair of coordinates.

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:17.900
Gemma 4 just came
out on Thursday.

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:20.140
And they have another
different kind of fun thing

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:21.980
that they do, which
they call, I think,

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:26.080
proportional RoPE or p-RoPE,
which is a really strange way

00:37:26.080 --> 00:37:28.580
to just say that the only thing
they rotate is the first two

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:30.820
coordinates, but that's
another valid thing that you

00:37:30.820 --> 00:37:31.560
can do as well.

00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:33.060
So there's a lot
of different things

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.620
that you can do in this
space that end up working.

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:36.420
OK.

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:40.020
In practice, what you're
going to end up doing is,

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:41.980
you can take your
vector, and you

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:45.480
can make a sparse multiply
with sines and cosines.

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:48.100
And this is going to be giving
you some way of rotating

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:49.850
your input vectors x's.

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:54.670
So x times w times r, this is
going to be your final embedding

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:57.750
that you get.

00:37:57.750 --> 00:37:59.890
And finally, this is
a sine and cosine,

00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:02.165
which looks a little
like sine embeddings.

00:38:02.165 --> 00:38:04.790
But it's really important that
I'm multiplying with these sines

00:38:04.790 --> 00:38:07.170
and cosines rather than
using them as embeddings,

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:09.350
because that means that
there are no cross terms.

00:38:09.350 --> 00:38:11.030
And this is purely relative.

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:12.710
There's no absolute
position information

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:15.773
that you'll get out
of inner products.

00:38:15.773 --> 00:38:18.190
If we really, really wanted
to get into low-level details,

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:20.470
and you ask, how do I
actually implement this thing?

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:22.670
You're going to have to do that.

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:24.730
You have your usual
attention stuff.

00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:27.870
And then what you do is you
generate cosine and sine angles

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:31.470
based on the position IDs
of where your sequence is.

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:33.510
And then you're going
to apply those cosines

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:37.390
and sines onto both your queries
and keys for your attention

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:38.365
computation.

00:38:38.365 --> 00:38:40.490
And you can either apply
them as a matrix multiply,

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:42.470
or you can go through
and apply them manually,

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:43.650
just as a rotation.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:44.770
Fairly straightforward.

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:46.910
And you would do this
at the attention level

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:49.320
rather than at the
very bottom, to enforce

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:51.120
position invariance,
every time you're

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:53.960
doing attention computations.

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:55.152
So that was RoPE.

00:38:55.152 --> 00:38:57.360
It is a little bit confusing,
but once you understand

00:38:57.360 --> 00:38:58.902
the geometry of just
rotating things,

00:38:58.902 --> 00:39:02.040
it's actually fairly
straightforward.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:02.600
OK.

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:05.480
I'm going to pause
here for one moment,

00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:09.200
in case anyone has questions
about the various architecture

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:10.080
bits.

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:13.200
We're going to then talk
about even lower level details

00:39:13.200 --> 00:39:16.800
about hyperparameters, so yes.

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.720
Do you know of any papers
that do a higher dimensional?

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:22.120
Higher dimensional rotation.

00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:23.920
It's a good question.

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:24.803
I don't think so.

00:39:24.803 --> 00:39:26.220
But a higher
dimensional rotation,

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:30.280
like any 2D rotation in the
space would just be a variant

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:31.100
of this.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.860
You could certainly do any one
manifold that is a closed loop.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:36.820
I have not seen that.

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:37.320
Yes.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:40.440
[INAUDIBLE] What do you
think is the best way

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:41.880
to distill this
kind of knowledge

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:45.680
from papers and
technical reports?

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.810
It's a good question.

00:39:47.810 --> 00:39:52.650
I don't know if there's a
way beyond some combination

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:55.530
of looking broadly enough
to get a pattern, which

00:39:55.530 --> 00:39:58.142
is what the procedure I'm trying
to do in this lecture here.

00:39:58.142 --> 00:40:00.850
And then the other one is to try
it yourself, even a much smaller

00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:03.890
scale, to form an intuition in a
theory for how these things come

00:40:03.890 --> 00:40:04.470
together.

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:06.470
I think those two are
really the right ways.

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:08.450
I think reading any
single paper in isolation

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:10.690
is very, very difficult,
especially now,

00:40:10.690 --> 00:40:14.410
because no single paper seems to
give any full detail for a lot

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:17.850
of language models these days.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:19.030
Oh, lots of questions now.

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:19.550
OK, good.

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:20.450
We'll go in.

00:40:20.450 --> 00:40:21.130
Yeah.

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:23.850
So I have a question
on the parallel layers

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:25.510
and [INAUDIBLE] layers.

00:40:25.510 --> 00:40:27.690
Yeah, I understand,
the modern models

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:30.350
are thinking of the
resource efficiency,

00:40:30.350 --> 00:40:32.370
so they use the parallel layers.

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:39.070
But there's a difference between
accuracy for these two patterns.

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:43.150
I want to know what's
the degree of accuracy.

00:40:43.150 --> 00:40:45.380
Is it big enough or
also small enough

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:47.460
to allow the current
model trainers

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:50.100
to ignore that, or [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:54.000
Yeah, I think that's
actually really mixed.

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:55.640
So if you read the
original PaLM paper,

00:40:55.640 --> 00:40:57.940
I think they're very
confident about the use

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:01.060
of parallel layers, like no
performance drop, 15% systems

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:02.360
utilization improvement.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:06.220
So if you read just that, you'll
say, oh, it's just as good.

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:08.460
But I think a lot of
the later Google models

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.430
have stopped using this,
which you can take on

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:12.180
as an implicit signal
that actually, there

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:13.940
might be some losses.

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:15.980
And once again, this
one is a little bit hard

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:18.840
to get precise numbers
on, because no one's

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:20.340
done the ablations,
as far as I know

00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:23.300
on parallel versus serial,
controlled nice ablations,

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:25.340
at least.

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:27.820
Yeah.

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:32.820
What's the difference between
P-RoPE and RoPE if you

00:41:32.820 --> 00:41:34.900
might have read [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:35.400
Yeah.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:35.520
Yeah.

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:37.420
I mean, this difference
is really just,

00:41:37.420 --> 00:41:39.080
which of the
coordinates you rotate?

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:42.100
You don't rotate most of
them, because a lot of the--

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:44.090
I mean, the argument
originally, I think,

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:48.450
is that the low frequency parts
just aren't rotating very much.

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:50.990
And so you can drop them
if you're really strapped

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:53.690
for of extra space.

00:41:53.690 --> 00:41:55.390
And this is really
an optimization

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:58.590
for teeny tiny models, where
you don't have very much hidden

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:02.230
dimensions to have
activations for.

00:42:02.230 --> 00:42:03.670
Yeah.

00:42:03.670 --> 00:42:08.830
So the relative embeddings,
not having an inner product.

00:42:08.830 --> 00:42:11.190
Is that because it only
applies to keys basically?

00:42:11.190 --> 00:42:12.590
I was trying to understand.

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:13.090
Yeah.

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:15.490
So they applied both
to the keys and values,

00:42:15.490 --> 00:42:19.270
which is why you get this
relative effect from where

00:42:19.270 --> 00:42:21.070
you are.

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:23.150
You want to not
have cross terms.

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:25.670
So if you look at the sine
and cosine embeddings,

00:42:25.670 --> 00:42:30.090
then you'll not only get
the original vectors,

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:32.550
you'll get these weird cross
terms between the position

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:35.790
embeddings and the word
embedding themselves,

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:37.130
and so on and so forth.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:39.830
And then you can back out
what the absolute position is.

00:42:39.830 --> 00:42:41.400
So even sine and
cosine embeddings

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:45.280
are not like pure relative
position embeddings.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:48.320
You have to accept the premise
that the relative embedding is

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:49.340
what you want.

00:42:49.340 --> 00:42:52.040
But once you do, you end
up at the RoPE solution

00:42:52.040 --> 00:42:53.880
somewhat naturally.

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:57.760
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:57.760 --> 00:43:00.920
Yeah, Yeah.

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:01.927
So what's the issue?

00:43:01.927 --> 00:43:03.760
So the issue with this
is that it just can't

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:05.220
be factorized as a product.

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.720
That's more of an
aesthetic problem.

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:10.820
If your constraints are,
I need it to be relative,

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:14.760
and I need it to factorize
as f of xi and f of yj,

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:17.200
then this is not a
solution in that class.

00:43:17.200 --> 00:43:19.560
To be fair, there's
a lot of embeddings

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:22.200
that work this way that
do work, like l of i

00:43:22.200 --> 00:43:26.640
and other kinds of approaches do
this inject into the attention

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:27.140
matrix.

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:30.200
And they do reasonably well.

00:43:30.200 --> 00:43:32.000
It's not necessarily
the one that's

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:36.060
become the dominant
approach, is what I can say.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:36.560
Cool.

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:38.000
OK.

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:40.490
Great.

00:43:40.490 --> 00:43:43.330
Now, we'll talk about
hyperparameters.

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:45.330
And I think hyperparameters
are really something

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:47.730
that you start to engage
with once you actually

00:43:47.730 --> 00:43:48.930
have to train a model.

00:43:48.930 --> 00:43:51.600
When your knowledge about
language models are abstract,

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:53.350
you don't have to care
about any of these.

00:43:53.350 --> 00:43:54.850
But once you have
to instantiate it,

00:43:54.850 --> 00:43:57.030
you start to ask
questions like, well,

00:43:57.030 --> 00:43:59.730
how big should the
feedforward size be?

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:01.850
How many heads should I have?

00:44:01.850 --> 00:44:04.995
What should my vocab size be?

00:44:04.995 --> 00:44:06.370
And you might also
have questions

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:08.950
of, what should my weight
decay or dropout be?

00:44:08.950 --> 00:44:10.190
Do I even need to regularize?

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:11.570
I have a lot of tokens.

00:44:11.570 --> 00:44:14.170
So do I need regularization?

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:16.670
And do I need very deep
models or very wide models?

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:19.150
What are the right kinds
of things to do here?

00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:21.983
And all of these, if you
start out with no knowledge,

00:44:21.983 --> 00:44:23.650
it's actually very
daunting, because you

00:44:23.650 --> 00:44:27.170
have to search this very
big high-dimensional space.

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:28.970
But the space of
things that people try

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:30.270
is actually pretty small.

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:31.687
And from that,
maybe you can start

00:44:31.687 --> 00:44:34.250
to think about smarter
search processes of where

00:44:34.250 --> 00:44:37.340
you want to vary things.

00:44:37.340 --> 00:44:40.820
One of the things that's a
really consensus hyperparameter

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:45.360
is this idea of the ratio
between the feedforward size,

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:47.340
which is the output
of your first matrix

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:50.580
multiply in an MLP and
the model dimension.

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:55.020
So this is really the ratio of
the two dimensions of your W1

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:57.745
as well your W2 matrix.

00:44:57.745 --> 00:44:59.620
This seems like a thing
that's very important

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:02.740
and controls the
richness of your MLPs.

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:04.060
So what should it be?

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:06.420
Well, for whatever
reason, it should maybe

00:45:06.420 --> 00:45:10.460
be four times your
hidden dimension.

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:14.160
And this is a rule of thumb
that works remarkably well.

00:45:14.160 --> 00:45:18.020
And I will show you some data on
why maybe this is a fine number

00:45:18.020 --> 00:45:18.520
to choose.

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.380
There's a few exceptions.

00:45:20.380 --> 00:45:22.740
And funnily enough, the
really extreme exceptions

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:25.060
backtrack on that.

00:45:25.060 --> 00:45:26.500
OK.

00:45:26.500 --> 00:45:30.350
Exception number 1 is variance
of the gated linear unit.

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:31.600
I already told you about this.

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:32.975
So if you were
thinking about it,

00:45:32.975 --> 00:45:35.120
this is probably cached
in your head right.

00:45:35.120 --> 00:45:38.980
GLUs have more parameters if
you keep the same dimensions.

00:45:38.980 --> 00:45:41.400
So if you want to keep the
parameter size of your MLPs

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:45.040
the same, well, you need
to scale down by 2/3.

00:45:45.040 --> 00:45:48.600
So most GLU variants, this means
that you're going to end up with

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:51.700
something like 2.67-ish.

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:56.340
So everyone that's
down here, 2.67 to 2.5,

00:45:56.340 --> 00:46:01.320
this is roughly applying
this 2/3 correction.

00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:06.300
And then for whatever reason,
the Llama 2 folks decided,

00:46:06.300 --> 00:46:10.080
well, we actually have very
efficient attention heads

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:15.680
with MQA, which I'll
talk about later.

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:18.280
And because of that, we
can multiply this ratio

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:20.080
by an arbitrary 1.33.

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:21.700
And we'll get roughly 3.5.

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:23.525
And so the Llama
people arbitrarily

00:46:23.525 --> 00:46:24.900
chose a slightly
different ratio,

00:46:24.900 --> 00:46:28.720
which essentially emphasizes
the MLPs a little bit more.

00:46:28.720 --> 00:46:31.500
But really, if you actually
look through all the papers,

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:36.210
you'll find either
2.6-ish or 3.5 for GLUs,

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:41.770
or 4 if you're doing
non-GLU models.

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:42.390
OK.

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:43.807
There's another
exception, which I

00:46:43.807 --> 00:46:46.250
find to be very funny,
but also very, very cool,

00:46:46.250 --> 00:46:50.670
which is, throughout, as you
read these technical reports,

00:46:50.670 --> 00:46:52.530
you'll find that
most people are just

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:54.830
very boring in their
choice of architectures.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:57.890
They're like, we did Llama,
but we changed one thing.

00:46:57.890 --> 00:47:01.770
But folks at Google are
very bold, sometimes.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:03.850
And T5 is one of
my favorite ones,

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:06.650
because they have some
really bold settings.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:10.950
They decided that instead of
following this 4x rule of thumb,

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:14.590
they decided that they want
to have a 64x multiplier,

00:47:14.590 --> 00:47:17.432
which is way bigger than 4.

00:47:17.432 --> 00:47:18.890
And they have a
reasonable argument

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:21.730
for this as well, this another
systems-based argument.

00:47:21.730 --> 00:47:25.070
They said, well, the bigger
my matrix multiplies,

00:47:25.070 --> 00:47:27.490
the more efficient I
can keep my hardware.

00:47:27.490 --> 00:47:30.940
So if I make this
multiplier really big,

00:47:30.940 --> 00:47:34.780
then my matrix multiplies
can potentially

00:47:34.780 --> 00:47:36.920
be more efficiently utilized.

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:41.180
And some others, like
Gemma 2, have also

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:44.600
tried to really push a
little bit higher on this.

00:47:44.600 --> 00:47:48.360
But really, T5 is
astounding exception at 64.

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:50.180
I don't think any
other model has really

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:54.940
gone that high in the
feed forward multiplier.

00:47:54.940 --> 00:47:58.580
And empirically, if you
look at other works that

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:01.580
try to do more controlled
comparisons of this ratio,

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:03.640
I've taken this one
from Kaplan in 2020.

00:48:03.640 --> 00:48:06.460
This is the classic
neural scaling loss

00:48:06.460 --> 00:48:11.660
paper, where they do various
controlled studies on language

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:12.380
models.

00:48:12.380 --> 00:48:14.900
You'll see, this wasn't
the point of the study.

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:16.040
It was a scaling law study.

00:48:16.040 --> 00:48:17.580
But you'll see in
one of the panels

00:48:17.580 --> 00:48:21.220
that they actually have ablation
or sweep, where they change

00:48:21.220 --> 00:48:23.520
the feed forward ratio,
and they look at the loss

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:25.520
for a very small model here.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:29.510
But what they find
in this paper is,

00:48:29.510 --> 00:48:31.830
there's a basin, where
you start at about one,

00:48:31.830 --> 00:48:33.830
and you end about
maybe 10, where

00:48:33.830 --> 00:48:36.810
this hyperparameter is pretty
good and very, very flat.

00:48:36.810 --> 00:48:40.730
You lose very little relative
to the optimal loss down here.

00:48:40.730 --> 00:48:45.070
And then if you get it really
wrong, you get above 10 to 100

00:48:45.070 --> 00:48:47.030
or something like
that, then your loss

00:48:47.030 --> 00:48:49.550
starts really shooting
up quadratically.

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:53.470
And so a lot of these choices
that range between 2.6 to 4,

00:48:53.470 --> 00:48:56.810
they're all falling into
this relatively nice basin,

00:48:56.810 --> 00:48:59.910
so you're fine choosing
those numbers right.

00:48:59.910 --> 00:49:03.170
So what can we learn
about this hyperparameter?

00:49:03.170 --> 00:49:05.270
Well, the default choices
have worked very well

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:07.290
for nearly all modern
language models.

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:09.350
So you can safely choose that.

00:49:09.350 --> 00:49:12.830
T5 was a fine model or the
version 1 T5 was a fine model.

00:49:12.830 --> 00:49:14.510
It wasn't a bad model.

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:17.510
And so even radical choices
can technically work,

00:49:17.510 --> 00:49:20.238
but it's probably going
to be compute-inefficient.

00:49:20.238 --> 00:49:22.030
And I think the funniest
part of this saga,

00:49:22.030 --> 00:49:24.610
this is the punchline
of the T5 saga to me,

00:49:24.610 --> 00:49:29.560
is that they have a follow up
model T5 v1.1 that's supposed

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:31.400
to be the improved
version of T5.

00:49:31.400 --> 00:49:34.760
And they go back to the
standard 2.5 multiplier.

00:49:34.760 --> 00:49:36.860
So there's nothing
explicitly stated here.

00:49:36.860 --> 00:49:39.453
But clearly, when they
tried to update T5,

00:49:39.453 --> 00:49:41.120
they decided that
they wanted to go back

00:49:41.120 --> 00:49:42.703
to a more standard
multiplier, which I

00:49:42.703 --> 00:49:45.840
find to be a little bit funny.

00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:49.960
So that's the feed forward
ratio, which now, you

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:53.480
have a rough sense of what the
right order of magnitude is.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:55.560
Now, let's talk about
a different consensus

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:57.120
hyperparameter.

00:49:57.120 --> 00:50:01.920
I always found this to be very
strange when teaching 224n

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:05.240
and just teaching students
about this, which is,

00:50:05.240 --> 00:50:06.840
if you have a
multihead attention,

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:09.200
where you have multiple
heads for your attention

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:12.340
in your transformer, the
canonical thing to do,

00:50:12.340 --> 00:50:14.680
the thing that almost
everyone does is,

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:18.720
if you have multiple heads, you
make sure that the size of those

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:22.240
heads, the head dimension is
such that you have the same

00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:24.400
dimension as a single
head transformer.

00:50:24.400 --> 00:50:29.490
So you always make sure that
you divide the hidden dimension

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:31.350
to basically, multiply with h.

00:50:31.350 --> 00:50:33.650
So in this case, you have
h the number of heads,

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:35.670
and the dimension of
each head is d over h.

00:50:35.670 --> 00:50:37.490
So you multiply the
two and you get d.

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:40.043
For some reason, this
is the rule of thumb.

00:50:40.043 --> 00:50:41.710
Of course, this doesn't
have to be true.

00:50:41.710 --> 00:50:44.770
We can arbitrarily change the
ratios between head dimensions

00:50:44.770 --> 00:50:46.070
and model dimensions.

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:48.550
But most models do
follow this guideline.

00:50:48.550 --> 00:50:51.515
And it turns out to
work pretty well.

00:50:51.515 --> 00:50:53.890
And we can look at a variety
of different models, classic

00:50:53.890 --> 00:50:54.390
and new.

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:57.230
I have the latest and
greatest coin as well.

00:50:57.230 --> 00:51:01.090
And you find, yeah, the ratios
are roughly around one a model

00:51:01.090 --> 00:51:05.930
head, notable exception of
T5 and even lambda, which

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:07.810
is another Google model.

00:51:07.810 --> 00:51:09.830
But really, everyone
sticks around one.

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:13.690
And I think this is
an interesting one.

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:16.810
I think the thing about
head dimensions that I'll

00:51:16.810 --> 00:51:18.450
end with here is,
I think this is yet

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:20.708
another forgiving
hyperparameter.

00:51:20.708 --> 00:51:22.750
There's a couple ablations
that people have done.

00:51:22.750 --> 00:51:25.620
There's, once again, a
pretty wide basin around one

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:28.020
that you can get away with.

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:31.860
But that one's maybe not the
most critical hyperparameter.

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:34.680
I think maybe one of the most
critical and interesting ones,

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:37.760
I think, conceptually, is
this idea of an aspect ratio.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:42.740
And to add an extra point
here, when you scale models

00:51:42.740 --> 00:51:44.460
up or down, the
way you usually do

00:51:44.460 --> 00:51:47.020
that is you fix an aspect
ratio, like how wide your model

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:48.480
is versus how deep it is.

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:50.020
And then you make
the model bigger.

00:51:50.020 --> 00:51:51.478
So the aspect ratio,
in some sense,

00:51:51.478 --> 00:51:54.540
controls the entire
depth to width trade off

00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:57.140
as you make models bigger.

00:51:57.140 --> 00:51:59.920
Now, you might wonder how
deep should my model be.

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:02.340
If you've been following
all this stuff on reasoning

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:04.100
and so on, you
might think I need

00:52:04.100 --> 00:52:06.140
a really deep model or
really shallow model

00:52:06.140 --> 00:52:08.120
if I want systems utilization.

00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:10.120
You might think that
there's a lot of variation.

00:52:10.120 --> 00:52:12.340
And there is a lot of
variation, much more so

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:14.080
than other hyperparameters.

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:16.860
But there's actually a
fairly clear sweet spot

00:52:16.860 --> 00:52:20.340
that most modern
models fall into.

00:52:20.340 --> 00:52:25.150
You don't really see
models go too deep.

00:52:25.150 --> 00:52:29.110
And you also don't see models
go too wide in either direction.

00:52:29.110 --> 00:52:32.990
You see, most models have a
ratio of about 100 D model

00:52:32.990 --> 00:52:34.670
over n layers.

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:39.110
So about 100s width for
every layer that you have.

00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:41.350
I mean, this is true
for GPT 3, or Llama,

00:52:41.350 --> 00:52:44.910
or any one of these models.

00:52:44.910 --> 00:52:47.910
And really, I think the
considerations are partly

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:50.530
a trade off between
expressiveness and hardware.

00:52:50.530 --> 00:52:53.830
If you have an
extremely deep model,

00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:56.530
they get very, very annoying
to deal with, systems-wise.

00:52:56.530 --> 00:52:58.790
The deeper your model,
what is the ways that you

00:52:58.790 --> 00:53:00.350
have for parallelizing them?

00:53:00.350 --> 00:53:02.310
Well, you might have
to cut up your layers.

00:53:02.310 --> 00:53:05.310
If you cut up your layers, we'll
talk about this in the systems

00:53:05.310 --> 00:53:05.810
lecture.

00:53:05.810 --> 00:53:09.070
Once you start cutting up
your layers depth-wise,

00:53:09.070 --> 00:53:11.610
you have very serious
issues in parallelization.

00:53:11.610 --> 00:53:13.770
Pipeline parallel, which
is what this is called,

00:53:13.770 --> 00:53:15.990
is something that most
people really, really

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:17.430
do not want to deal with.

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:19.750
Whereas width is much
easier to parallelize.

00:53:19.750 --> 00:53:23.640
If you have a really wide model,
you can cut that up very easily

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:24.700
in your GPUs.

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:26.200
Tensor parallel is
what it's called.

00:53:26.200 --> 00:53:29.520
It's much, much
simpler to deal with.

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:32.780
And so in some sense, there
are systems reasons to go wide.

00:53:32.780 --> 00:53:35.200
And maybe there's expressiveness
reasons to go deep.

00:53:35.200 --> 00:53:39.000
And you end up at roughly 100.

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:41.520
And I think one of the
really interesting things

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:44.537
about transformer
hyperparameters

00:53:44.537 --> 00:53:46.120
is, there are a lot
of hyperparameters

00:53:46.120 --> 00:53:49.100
that seem quite important, but
they're also fairly forgiving.

00:53:49.100 --> 00:53:51.640
And people have converged
roughly on the minimum.

00:53:51.640 --> 00:53:53.480
This is yet another
plot from Kaplan,

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:56.560
et al, which shows another
sweep over hyperparameters

00:53:56.560 --> 00:53:58.760
for differently sized models.

00:53:58.760 --> 00:54:00.560
And once again,
you see, regardless

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:03.540
of the size of your
model, roughly speaking,

00:54:03.540 --> 00:54:06.820
the optimum aspect
ratio is fairly similar,

00:54:06.820 --> 00:54:09.702
and they live at about hundreds,
maybe a little bit less,

00:54:09.702 --> 00:54:11.660
depending on how you want
to do the accounting.

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:15.160
But really, anywhere near
100 is a pretty safe bet

00:54:15.160 --> 00:54:18.160
for aspect ratios

00:54:18.160 --> 00:54:25.570
EK and others did a number of
really interesting architecture

00:54:25.570 --> 00:54:29.770
variation experiments, in which
their general conclusion on this

00:54:29.770 --> 00:54:32.350
was that, let's look
at the top panel here,

00:54:32.350 --> 00:54:35.250
you have a lot of
different kinds of models

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:37.690
that you can have in terms
of depth to width trade offs.

00:54:37.690 --> 00:54:40.390
But as you sweep the
depth to width trade offs,

00:54:40.390 --> 00:54:43.890
you find that really, the
only thing that matters,

00:54:43.890 --> 00:54:45.030
in some sense, is flops.

00:54:45.030 --> 00:54:47.270
As you increase the flops,
the models get better.

00:54:47.270 --> 00:54:49.890
And that's really controlling
the majority of the effects, not

00:54:49.890 --> 00:54:52.350
necessarily the aspect ratio.

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:54.770
And so I think what has
really emerged from this

00:54:54.770 --> 00:54:57.490
is the sense that there's
a general forgiving band

00:54:57.490 --> 00:54:59.910
of hyperparameters that
people tend to choose.

00:54:59.910 --> 00:55:02.450
And then you really worry
about primarily, your system's

00:55:02.450 --> 00:55:05.610
utilization rather than
expressiveness concerns,

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:08.350
which are hard to reason about.

00:55:08.350 --> 00:55:08.850
Cool.

00:55:11.410 --> 00:55:14.130
And then maybe the last
hyperparameter thing

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:18.190
that I want to mention
is vocabulary sizes.

00:55:18.190 --> 00:55:20.230
And this one's
interesting to me,

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:21.990
because there's a
really clear difference

00:55:21.990 --> 00:55:24.590
between two classes of models.

00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:32.550
I think, in the early days of
open source model training,

00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:34.190
there were a lot of
monolingual models,

00:55:34.190 --> 00:55:36.590
whose only goal was
to be good on English.

00:55:36.590 --> 00:55:40.150
And for those models, you had
these much smaller vocab sizes,

00:55:40.150 --> 00:55:42.390
in the 30,000 range.

00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:45.230
And then post Llama,
a lot of people

00:55:45.230 --> 00:55:47.510
were really interested in
multilingual or production

00:55:47.510 --> 00:55:51.030
systems, these include closed
source models like GPT 4.

00:55:51.030 --> 00:55:53.410
All of these have much,
much larger vocab sizes.

00:55:53.410 --> 00:55:58.510
And these are roughly in the
100,000 to 200,000 vocab range.

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:00.270
And you see, generally,
that Google models

00:56:00.270 --> 00:56:02.230
have a ton more vocab.

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:06.530
Llama derivatives roughly
range at about 100,000 tokens.

00:56:06.530 --> 00:56:10.950
And then the monolingual
models are about 30,000.

00:56:10.950 --> 00:56:12.130
This is somewhat clear.

00:56:12.130 --> 00:56:14.630
The multilingual models really
do need much larger vocab

00:56:14.630 --> 00:56:16.135
to cover the whole space.

00:56:16.135 --> 00:56:18.260
Generally, the models on
the right are also bigger.

00:56:18.260 --> 00:56:19.760
There have been
scaling law studies,

00:56:19.760 --> 00:56:22.600
showing that the bigger your
model, the larger the vocab

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:23.540
it can handle.

00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:25.240
And so this is also
partially driven

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:27.960
by modern scaling trends,
where the models on the right

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:28.900
are generally bigger.

00:56:28.900 --> 00:56:32.980
No ones training large
monolingual models anymore.

00:56:35.520 --> 00:56:38.160
So yeah.

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:45.960
[INAUDIBLE] the style stuff.

00:56:45.960 --> 00:56:48.880
How to use those vocab sizes?

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:52.800
Sorry, the question was, if you
have multilingual models or--

00:56:52.800 --> 00:56:53.640
Multimodal.

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:54.420
Multimodal.

00:56:54.420 --> 00:56:54.920
Yeah.

00:56:54.920 --> 00:56:57.480
So I guess, it
depends on the way

00:56:57.480 --> 00:56:58.700
that your tokens are encoded.

00:56:58.700 --> 00:57:01.117
But if you're tokenizing your
images and things like that,

00:57:01.117 --> 00:57:04.320
then you need to have many more
tokens to account for those.

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:06.173
If you look at various
open source releases,

00:57:06.173 --> 00:57:07.840
they'll have a different
image tokenizer

00:57:07.840 --> 00:57:10.760
with its own vocab,
which is quite large.

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:12.040
Yeah.

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:17.050
How valid is it to
compare, I suppose,

00:57:17.050 --> 00:57:21.810
this provides for
different [INAUDIBLE]?

00:57:21.810 --> 00:57:23.310
How valid is it to compare bits?

00:57:23.310 --> 00:57:24.230
Oh, that is a great question.

00:57:24.230 --> 00:57:24.490
OK.

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:25.008
Yeah.

00:57:25.008 --> 00:57:26.550
That's not a
hyperparameter question,

00:57:26.550 --> 00:57:27.810
but that's a good question.

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:30.470
So what is the right way?

00:57:30.470 --> 00:57:35.210
So let me step back a moment
and put us in the right mindset.

00:57:35.210 --> 00:57:37.610
So if we think about
language modeling,

00:57:37.610 --> 00:57:40.130
language modeling is a
generative modeling task.

00:57:40.130 --> 00:57:42.410
We are modeling the
probability of a sequence.

00:57:42.410 --> 00:57:45.390
Now, as long as your
sequence is fixed,

00:57:45.390 --> 00:57:47.490
you have adulterated it anyway.

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:50.110
And you provide a
probability over all strings,

00:57:50.110 --> 00:57:51.930
that's always valid to compare.

00:57:51.930 --> 00:57:54.170
At that level of
things is always valid.

00:57:54.170 --> 00:57:56.050
Now, when you ask
the question, is

00:57:56.050 --> 00:57:58.330
it valid to compare
the bits per byte

00:57:58.330 --> 00:58:02.690
of two arbitrary tokenizers?

00:58:02.690 --> 00:58:04.430
Really, there's
two things at play.

00:58:04.430 --> 00:58:07.230
The one thing is, did you
touch the sequence at all?

00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:09.450
If you look at some
tokenizers in the past

00:58:09.450 --> 00:58:12.340
before subword tokenizers,
they would drop some tokens

00:58:12.340 --> 00:58:14.360
or drop some words.

00:58:14.360 --> 00:58:16.080
That makes the
comparisons invalid.

00:58:16.080 --> 00:58:17.640
But modern tokenizers
are complete.

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:20.540
They can model any sequence,
so that's not a concern.

00:58:20.540 --> 00:58:22.420
The other thing that
you have to worry about

00:58:22.420 --> 00:58:25.240
is, are we length
normalizing in any way?

00:58:25.240 --> 00:58:26.980
But for bits per
byte, you're always

00:58:26.980 --> 00:58:28.720
normalizing with
the same number,

00:58:28.720 --> 00:58:29.960
which is the number of bytes.

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:31.780
And so this is always
a valid comparison.

00:58:31.780 --> 00:58:34.820
So that's how to think
about tokenizer comparisons.

00:58:34.820 --> 00:58:36.700
So for example, I
think there have

00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:40.780
been results showing that
comparing perplexity for a fixed

00:58:40.780 --> 00:58:47.100
tokenizers always leads to
better downstream performance

00:58:47.100 --> 00:58:52.820
on downstream tasks, as the same
thing improving for [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:52.820 --> 00:58:55.200
Perplexity and BPP are
dual to each other.

00:58:55.200 --> 00:58:58.700
So yes, if that's
what you're asking.

00:58:58.700 --> 00:59:01.760
It's only yes and it's only no,
because if you're comparing--

00:59:04.540 --> 00:59:08.260
you could train compared to
perplexity as [INAUDIBLE],

00:59:08.260 --> 00:59:10.370
but you're changing
a different thing.

00:59:10.370 --> 00:59:12.230
[INAUDIBLE]

00:59:12.230 --> 00:59:12.990
OK.

00:59:12.990 --> 00:59:14.270
We'll have to talk
later, because I'm not

00:59:14.270 --> 00:59:15.562
sure I understand the question.

00:59:15.562 --> 00:59:17.730
But I think that's an
interesting set of questions.

00:59:17.730 --> 00:59:18.250
OK, good.

00:59:18.250 --> 00:59:18.750
OK.

00:59:18.750 --> 00:59:21.830
All right.

00:59:21.830 --> 00:59:27.532
So we're going through, really,
the lowest levels of details

00:59:27.532 --> 00:59:28.990
of language modeling,
which I think

00:59:28.990 --> 00:59:31.550
really exposes a lot
of interesting ideas

00:59:31.550 --> 00:59:33.150
while we talk through this.

00:59:33.150 --> 00:59:35.950
And I think dropout is one
of the end regularization.

00:59:35.950 --> 00:59:39.530
I think it's another very
interesting class of ideas,

00:59:39.530 --> 00:59:41.950
also one that I think
is very counterintuitive

00:59:41.950 --> 00:59:44.790
from your Machine
Learning 101 intuition.

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:49.270
So let's go through what I
think is the standard argument

00:59:49.270 --> 00:59:51.790
for regularization.

00:59:51.790 --> 00:59:54.590
Well, if I'm doing language
modeling, I have a lot of data.

00:59:54.590 --> 00:59:57.250
I have more data than I can
process most of the time.

00:59:57.250 --> 01:00:00.150
Unless you're at
Google, maybe even then,

01:00:00.150 --> 01:00:03.070
There is more internet
data than there is flops.

01:00:03.070 --> 01:00:08.080
So I'm probably not even going
to see the same data twice.

01:00:08.080 --> 01:00:10.540
So I'm only going to do a
single pass on a corpus.

01:00:10.540 --> 01:00:12.800
And there's very good
reasons and arguments

01:00:12.800 --> 01:00:16.160
to believe that a single pass
of SGD or other optimizers

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.600
is never really going to
memorize my data very much.

01:00:19.600 --> 01:00:23.200
So this means overfitting
is not really a problem,

01:00:23.200 --> 01:00:26.960
almost ever during compute
constrained language modeling.

01:00:26.960 --> 01:00:30.680
Now, some people even actually
only look at training loss,

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:33.360
because they believe so strongly
that overfitting doesn't

01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:35.320
happen in single pass SGD.

01:00:35.320 --> 01:00:38.720
Now, given this, you can
sit and think about this.

01:00:38.720 --> 01:00:41.240
Should I use dropout
or weight decay

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:42.700
in language model training?

01:00:42.700 --> 01:00:47.840
You can think about it a bit.

01:00:47.840 --> 01:00:50.600
One unfortunate thing is
that a lot of recent models

01:00:50.600 --> 01:00:52.440
don't talk about
this stuff at all.

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:56.240
It's really lower level
details than tech reports

01:00:56.240 --> 01:00:58.400
are willing to expose.

01:00:58.400 --> 01:01:02.160
But if you look, actually,
you find a lot of models

01:01:02.160 --> 01:01:04.640
do both, especially
weight decay actually

01:01:04.640 --> 01:01:07.650
is a fairly popular
intervention even

01:01:07.650 --> 01:01:11.970
for modern high-performance
language models.

01:01:11.970 --> 01:01:14.250
This is very, very surprising.

01:01:14.250 --> 01:01:17.530
I mean, some of
the dropout things

01:01:17.530 --> 01:01:22.570
maybe have gone out of favor,
but weightdecay actually remains

01:01:22.570 --> 01:01:23.470
fairly popular.

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:24.750
And this is very mystifying.

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:26.650
Why is this?

01:01:26.650 --> 01:01:28.210
And this is one
of the reasons why

01:01:28.210 --> 01:01:29.630
I think deep learning is hard.

01:01:29.630 --> 01:01:33.170
And this architecture lecture
is very strange and hard.

01:01:33.170 --> 01:01:36.230
It's because these things
interact in very strange ways.

01:01:36.230 --> 01:01:39.890
So there have been papers
that have argued and shown

01:01:39.890 --> 01:01:43.330
nice evidence that
weight decay is actually

01:01:43.330 --> 01:01:45.330
not a regularizer, sometimes.

01:01:45.330 --> 01:01:52.290
It actually interacts with the
optimizer to essentially make

01:01:52.290 --> 01:01:54.530
optimization better.

01:01:54.530 --> 01:01:57.610
So if you look at the
training versus validation

01:01:57.610 --> 01:01:59.770
loss across different
weight decay settings

01:01:59.770 --> 01:02:02.780
on language model training
for single pass SGD,

01:02:02.780 --> 01:02:04.280
you don't really
see any difference.

01:02:04.280 --> 01:02:05.985
Weight decay isn't
shifting things,

01:02:05.985 --> 01:02:07.360
so the validation
loss is better.

01:02:07.360 --> 01:02:08.652
There's already no overfitting.

01:02:08.652 --> 01:02:10.660
Run the x equals y line here.

01:02:10.660 --> 01:02:14.060
So doesn't control
overfitting, but if we

01:02:14.060 --> 01:02:17.260
look at different
levels of weight decay,

01:02:17.260 --> 01:02:19.560
and not only just different
levels of weight decay,

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:24.500
we look at weight decay combined
with learning rate decay.

01:02:24.500 --> 01:02:27.580
What we find is that the
stronger weight decay runs,

01:02:27.580 --> 01:02:30.220
these blue dashed
lines on the bottom

01:02:30.220 --> 01:02:33.860
do significantly better,
because they start out slow,

01:02:33.860 --> 01:02:37.340
but they essentially
end up converging

01:02:37.340 --> 01:02:39.240
to a much better minimum later.

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:42.440
And this is generally true
when we decay learning rate,

01:02:42.440 --> 01:02:45.580
not necessarily true when we're
in constant learning rate, which

01:02:45.580 --> 01:02:49.580
is maybe somewhat more of where
your intuition is coming from.

01:02:49.580 --> 01:02:53.220
So this is part of why it's
very difficult to reason,

01:02:53.220 --> 01:02:56.940
a priori or from scratch,
the behavior of all

01:02:56.940 --> 01:02:59.100
these different
choices and why I

01:02:59.100 --> 01:03:01.820
think Percy and I have designed
this class, so that you interact

01:03:01.820 --> 01:03:05.430
with stuff, because you
might come upon this thing

01:03:05.430 --> 01:03:07.910
where basically, weight decay
is actually an optimization

01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:12.050
intervention and not necessarily
a regularization intervention,

01:03:12.050 --> 01:03:13.830
which is what you
would expect here.

01:03:13.830 --> 01:03:16.230
So always keep that in
mind that these kinds

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:19.110
of unexpected effects
can really start to kick

01:03:19.110 --> 01:03:24.270
in for these kinds of settings.

01:03:24.270 --> 01:03:26.150
Cool.

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:26.830
All right.

01:03:26.830 --> 01:03:29.850
So to put everything
together for hyperparameters,

01:03:29.850 --> 01:03:33.150
there's actually, for a lot of
the maybe more hairy looking

01:03:33.150 --> 01:03:36.350
hyperparameters, actually just
fairly standard choices that

01:03:36.350 --> 01:03:38.910
have worked well for everybody.

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:41.350
Factor of 4 rule of
thumb, keep your head dim

01:03:41.350 --> 01:03:45.390
and your number of heads
equal to the model dimension.

01:03:45.390 --> 01:03:48.790
Pick an aspect ratio,
roughly around 100.

01:03:48.790 --> 01:03:51.462
And if you ask about
regularization,

01:03:51.462 --> 01:03:53.170
you want to maybe try
a couple of things,

01:03:53.170 --> 01:03:55.710
because regularization
actually does interact

01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:58.550
with optimizers in ways that
are quite counterintuitive.

01:03:58.550 --> 01:04:01.020
So this is the thing that
some people still do,

01:04:01.020 --> 01:04:04.273
even though you don't need
the regularization at all.

01:04:04.273 --> 01:04:05.940
Actually, maybe I'll
stop here, in case.

01:04:05.940 --> 01:04:07.185
Yeah.

01:04:07.185 --> 01:04:09.560
Are there any significant
architectural differences maybe

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:12.840
for diffusion model?

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:14.280
Diffusions.

01:04:14.280 --> 01:04:16.995
That, I have not looked
into enough, to be honest.

01:04:16.995 --> 01:04:18.620
There aren't that
many people training.

01:04:18.620 --> 01:04:20.417
Big diffusion is one issue.

01:04:20.417 --> 01:04:23.000
And many of the models that have
been trained are retrofitted.

01:04:23.000 --> 01:04:24.708
So I think the
architectures are actually

01:04:24.708 --> 01:04:26.932
the same as a Llama-like model.

01:04:26.932 --> 01:04:28.640
But if you're asking
the question, what's

01:04:28.640 --> 01:04:31.140
the optimal architecture if
you were to train from scratch,

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:32.640
I don't know what
that is, actually,

01:04:32.640 --> 01:04:34.040
off the top of my head.

01:04:34.040 --> 01:04:34.640
Yeah.

01:04:34.640 --> 01:04:37.880
Do you have any explanation
for why regularization mostly

01:04:37.880 --> 01:04:39.240
affects [INAUDIBLE]?

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:41.600
Well, I guess, it's not that
regularization, in general,

01:04:41.600 --> 01:04:42.420
affects optimization.

01:04:42.420 --> 01:04:44.087
I don't think people
do dropout anymore,

01:04:44.087 --> 01:04:47.020
because it doesn't really
interact well with optimization.

01:04:47.020 --> 01:04:50.475
But for example, weight decay,
which is shrinkage to 0,

01:04:50.475 --> 01:04:52.600
that might allow you to
use a higher learning rate,

01:04:52.600 --> 01:04:54.620
or it might allow
you to decay faster.

01:04:54.620 --> 01:04:56.703
There are lots of ways in
which all of these terms

01:04:56.703 --> 01:04:59.240
are interrelated.

01:04:59.240 --> 01:04:59.740
Cool.

01:04:59.740 --> 01:05:01.980
OK.

01:05:01.980 --> 01:05:06.620
Now, I've talked a lot about
how to design expressive models

01:05:06.620 --> 01:05:10.220
by looking at all these other
models that have been trained.

01:05:10.220 --> 01:05:12.380
One of the things that
I'll highlight now

01:05:12.380 --> 01:05:16.300
is, over the last few
years, a really big emphasis

01:05:16.300 --> 01:05:18.280
has not been on
performance alone,

01:05:18.280 --> 01:05:20.260
it has actually
been on stability.

01:05:20.260 --> 01:05:22.940
And this becomes an
increasingly important concern

01:05:22.940 --> 01:05:26.040
as your models get more and
more expensive to train.

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:29.300
We've seen that a lot of
these choices are forgiving.

01:05:29.300 --> 01:05:31.460
Everyone's doing similar stuff.

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:33.690
And so you can mess with
these, but you're not

01:05:33.690 --> 01:05:35.440
going to get a big
performance difference.

01:05:35.440 --> 01:05:36.420
That's fine.

01:05:36.420 --> 01:05:40.120
But if your model suddenly blows
up some part into training,

01:05:40.120 --> 01:05:42.900
you get these horrible-looking
spikes all over the place,

01:05:42.900 --> 01:05:46.460
you might end up with a
model that is actually

01:05:46.460 --> 01:05:48.260
not very good quality.

01:05:48.260 --> 01:05:49.680
Or it might be unrecoverable.

01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:52.100
You might have spent millions
of dollars in training.

01:05:52.100 --> 01:05:54.580
And you get to a point
where the model is no longer

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:55.900
able to be trained any further.

01:05:55.900 --> 01:05:57.690
That would be a
horrible thing to happen

01:05:57.690 --> 01:06:00.390
if you have a lot of compute
that you want to spend.

01:06:00.390 --> 01:06:01.870
So you don't want
to train models

01:06:01.870 --> 01:06:04.630
that look like this blue
curve with spikes everywhere

01:06:04.630 --> 01:06:07.430
and these big gradient
norms happening.

01:06:07.430 --> 01:06:10.170
So what do we do to fix
these stability issues?

01:06:10.170 --> 01:06:13.750
I mean, this is really, I
would say, a core, core issue.

01:06:13.750 --> 01:06:18.070
And if you have stability
issues in language models,

01:06:18.070 --> 01:06:20.470
or in general, neural
networks, there's

01:06:20.470 --> 01:06:24.990
a few usual suspects that
you've got to start looking at.

01:06:24.990 --> 01:06:26.970
One of them is the softmaxes.

01:06:26.970 --> 01:06:29.830
And the softmax has two things
that are both really bad

01:06:29.830 --> 01:06:32.150
for stability-- one of
them is an exponential.

01:06:32.150 --> 01:06:34.550
You can see how that
blows up very quickly.

01:06:34.550 --> 01:06:36.170
You also divide two numbers.

01:06:36.170 --> 01:06:39.210
And that's also a potentially
very dangerous operation.

01:06:39.210 --> 01:06:42.550
So softmax is one place
where you got to be extra,

01:06:42.550 --> 01:06:44.030
extra careful.

01:06:44.030 --> 01:06:46.370
And where are the softmaxes
in a language model?

01:06:46.370 --> 01:06:47.910
Well, there's two of them.

01:06:47.910 --> 01:06:50.830
There's one on the output side,
when we output our probability

01:06:50.830 --> 01:06:51.850
distribution.

01:06:51.850 --> 01:06:54.150
And then in attention, when
we normalize the attention,

01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:56.080
there's going to
be another softmax.

01:06:56.080 --> 01:06:57.880
So we can think of
both of those as really

01:06:57.880 --> 01:07:02.780
danger zones for our model,
especially the attention.

01:07:06.680 --> 01:07:09.220
Let's start with thinking
about the output softmax.

01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:12.480
The output softmax
can blow up on us.

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:14.520
And one of the
things that we can do

01:07:14.520 --> 01:07:19.220
is, we can try to control
the normalizer problem.

01:07:19.220 --> 01:07:22.460
So let's think about
the softmax calculation.

01:07:22.460 --> 01:07:25.560
We want to compute a log
probability to compute the loss.

01:07:25.560 --> 01:07:27.020
Now, what is the
log probability?

01:07:27.020 --> 01:07:29.840
Well, it's the output
of your model u.

01:07:29.840 --> 01:07:32.820
And then you've got
this log normalizer.

01:07:32.820 --> 01:07:35.005
This u is well-behaved,
because in some sense,

01:07:35.005 --> 01:07:36.380
this is the output
of your model.

01:07:36.380 --> 01:07:38.720
This is just the output of
your residual stream with all

01:07:38.720 --> 01:07:39.928
the things that are added in.

01:07:39.928 --> 01:07:43.200
So if u is well-behaved,
then the first term

01:07:43.200 --> 01:07:45.520
is well-behaved, if
the model is being OK.

01:07:45.520 --> 01:07:49.940
Now, the second term, this log
z, this might not be so OK.

01:07:49.940 --> 01:07:52.400
If z is really big
or really small,

01:07:52.400 --> 01:07:55.042
even if the output of your
model is somewhat well-behaved,

01:07:55.042 --> 01:07:55.750
it could blow up.

01:07:55.750 --> 01:07:56.333
And what is z?

01:07:56.333 --> 01:07:57.610
Well, it's an exponential.

01:07:57.610 --> 01:08:00.290
So it could potentially
blow up very quickly on you.

01:08:00.290 --> 01:08:02.290
Or if this is 0, it could
also blow up on you.

01:08:02.290 --> 01:08:04.890
So both of those directions
are very, very bad.

01:08:04.890 --> 01:08:10.450
Now, we would ideally like
our z to be somewhere near 1

01:08:10.450 --> 01:08:13.450
or log z to be somewhere near 0.

01:08:13.450 --> 01:08:14.650
What can we do?

01:08:14.650 --> 01:08:16.529
Well, one of the
things that you notice,

01:08:16.529 --> 01:08:19.850
if you thought about the
action of the softmax,

01:08:19.850 --> 01:08:23.170
is, this whole thing
is overparametrized.

01:08:23.170 --> 01:08:27.130
I could push things in and out,
so if I add a constant to you,

01:08:27.130 --> 01:08:29.970
I can manipulate the z's
without really affecting

01:08:29.970 --> 01:08:31.229
the output of the softmax.

01:08:31.229 --> 01:08:33.490
It can cancel out
between the normalizer

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:35.810
and the output of my model.

01:08:35.810 --> 01:08:38.529
So because of that property,
one thing that I could do

01:08:38.529 --> 01:08:40.170
is I could add a regularizer.

01:08:40.170 --> 01:08:44.569
This is from Jacob Devlin's
paper, 2024-- sorry, 2014,

01:08:44.569 --> 01:08:49.210
in which he adds this
squared log z term.

01:08:49.210 --> 01:08:52.500
And what this is doing is
it's just penalizing how far

01:08:52.500 --> 01:08:55.120
away your log z is from 0.

01:08:55.120 --> 01:08:57.140
And if log z is
near 0, that's nice,

01:08:57.140 --> 01:09:01.500
because this whole expression
is numerically stable.

01:09:01.500 --> 01:09:03.819
This is called the z loss trick.

01:09:03.819 --> 01:09:06.180
It's been used by
a number of papers.

01:09:06.180 --> 01:09:10.779
Jacob Devlin and others
initially pioneered this back

01:09:10.779 --> 01:09:12.040
in 2014.

01:09:12.040 --> 01:09:14.220
And then it's become popular
again through a number

01:09:14.220 --> 01:09:15.580
of open source models.

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:18.279
Baichuan, I think, was the first
open source model to do it,

01:09:18.279 --> 01:09:20.020
but then DCLM and
OLMo, and others

01:09:20.020 --> 01:09:22.600
have been using this trick
to stabilize their output

01:09:22.600 --> 01:09:23.740
softmaxes.

01:09:23.740 --> 01:09:26.439
So this is a surprisingly
effective thing.

01:09:27.840 --> 01:09:30.540
So let's say, we've handled
the instability issues

01:09:30.540 --> 01:09:32.963
on the output softmax.

01:09:32.963 --> 01:09:34.380
Now, we have to
turn our attention

01:09:34.380 --> 01:09:37.720
towards the other potential
problem, which is attention.

01:09:37.720 --> 01:09:41.600
And this is a place where
lots of degeneracies happen.

01:09:41.600 --> 01:09:43.420
Lots of techniques
have been developed

01:09:43.420 --> 01:09:48.580
to control the instability that
attention operations generate.

01:09:48.580 --> 01:09:52.149
And really, the high
level thing that I'll say

01:09:52.149 --> 01:09:54.630
is, if you have
instability, if you

01:09:54.630 --> 01:09:57.850
can throw a layer norm in there,
somehow, it might control it.

01:09:57.850 --> 01:09:59.390
And that's really,
in some sense,

01:09:59.390 --> 01:10:04.550
the design philosophy behind
this idea called the QK norm.

01:10:04.550 --> 01:10:09.230
So what you do is remember
that we have our Qs and Ks that

01:10:09.230 --> 01:10:10.993
are going to be
multiplied together,

01:10:10.993 --> 01:10:12.910
and then they're going
to go into the softmax.

01:10:12.910 --> 01:10:14.910
So in the standard
attention operation,

01:10:14.910 --> 01:10:17.310
I'm going to layer norm
as a prelayer norm,

01:10:17.310 --> 01:10:18.930
multiply with a QKV.

01:10:18.930 --> 01:10:20.710
And I'm going to
get my Qs and Ks.

01:10:20.710 --> 01:10:22.910
Those will get multiplied
by a matrix multiply,

01:10:22.910 --> 01:10:25.790
I'll softmax them, and
I'll multiply that with a V

01:10:25.790 --> 01:10:27.130
to get the weighted average.

01:10:27.130 --> 01:10:29.130
And then I'll output
whatever comes after right.

01:10:29.130 --> 01:10:31.030
So this is our usual attention.

01:10:31.030 --> 01:10:34.510
Now, what happens if we
just throw in a LayerNorm

01:10:34.510 --> 01:10:37.710
before we multiply
the Qs and Ks?

01:10:37.710 --> 01:10:41.870
If we do that, then that the
inputs to this matrix multiply

01:10:41.870 --> 01:10:45.070
and therefore, the inputs
to the softmax, roughly

01:10:45.070 --> 01:10:46.210
have the same scale.

01:10:46.210 --> 01:10:48.900
They're always going to
have a scale of roughly one,

01:10:48.900 --> 01:10:53.280
because we've used the RMSE norm
to divide the size of those Qs

01:10:53.280 --> 01:10:55.400
and Ks.

01:10:55.400 --> 01:10:58.640
If we do that, then
we're going to keep

01:10:58.640 --> 01:11:00.460
this softmax operation stable.

01:11:00.460 --> 01:11:01.900
Tons of different
models do this.

01:11:01.900 --> 01:11:06.560
It's originally from
the multimodal world.

01:11:06.560 --> 01:11:09.360
Some folks who were
making multimodal models

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:14.400
initially discovered QK norm,
Idefcs, and Chameleon really

01:11:14.400 --> 01:11:16.340
use this and proved it out.

01:11:16.340 --> 01:11:19.640
And then a number of other
open source language models

01:11:19.640 --> 01:11:21.600
realize that the same
tricks are entirely

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:26.140
applicable to stabilizing
attention for language models.

01:11:26.140 --> 01:11:28.060
And I think this is now
very, very standard.

01:11:28.060 --> 01:11:30.640
QK norm is actually a
very standard intervention

01:11:30.640 --> 01:11:34.040
that most of the large
models now introduce.

01:11:34.040 --> 01:11:36.400
It doesn't seem to
affect performance

01:11:36.400 --> 01:11:38.180
from lots of different
training runs,

01:11:38.180 --> 01:11:40.640
but it does definitely
prevent the kinds

01:11:40.640 --> 01:11:43.400
of attention degeneracies.

01:11:43.400 --> 01:11:47.290
And really, the way
that I've seen this is,

01:11:47.290 --> 01:11:50.270
we have layer norms,
initially in the prenorm.

01:11:50.270 --> 01:11:54.430
Now, we add them after the
nonlinearities in each block.

01:11:54.430 --> 01:11:56.790
And now, we're throwing them
in both the Qs and the Ks.

01:11:56.790 --> 01:12:01.010
And really, I think this is
getting at the stabilization

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:03.010
tricks that people
apply to this world.

01:12:03.010 --> 01:12:07.410
Now, the final set of
things that I'll talk about

01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:10.490
as a stability intervention,
and frankly, this one

01:12:10.490 --> 01:12:14.890
is not as popular and more
of a Google-specific trick

01:12:14.890 --> 01:12:18.690
that I've seen, but
logit soft-capping

01:12:18.690 --> 01:12:21.550
is a much harder intervention
that some people apply.

01:12:21.550 --> 01:12:25.570
So this one, in QK
norm, what we're doing

01:12:25.570 --> 01:12:28.210
is we are controlling
the inputs to the softmax

01:12:28.210 --> 01:12:30.730
and hoping that the
outputs are well-behaved.

01:12:30.730 --> 01:12:35.190
If we really, really want to
enforce well-behaved outputs,

01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:38.090
what we can do is we can take
the logits, the things that

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:40.930
go straight into the softmax,
and we can just cap them off,

01:12:40.930 --> 01:12:43.690
so they can never be
too large or too small.

01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:45.863
This is almost a
hard constraint.

01:12:45.863 --> 01:12:47.280
It's called a soft
cap, of course,

01:12:47.280 --> 01:12:51.300
but a Tanh is bounded
at some value.

01:12:51.300 --> 01:12:54.060
And so this is in
the Gemma models.

01:12:54.060 --> 01:13:00.780
I think Gemma 2, 3, and 4 all
use the logit soft cap trick.

01:13:00.780 --> 01:13:02.780
And what they do
is they take all

01:13:02.780 --> 01:13:05.080
of their logits for
the attention layers,

01:13:05.080 --> 01:13:08.660
and then they soft cap
them at some value.

01:13:08.660 --> 01:13:11.140
Some NVIDIA folks have done
actually quite nice work

01:13:11.140 --> 01:13:14.980
doing systematic comparisons of
these stability interventions.

01:13:14.980 --> 01:13:19.400
And what they find is, if you
start with a baseline model,

01:13:19.400 --> 01:13:21.400
you can do all sorts of
different interventions.

01:13:21.400 --> 01:13:23.708
And QK norm is here.

01:13:23.708 --> 01:13:25.500
And it does slightly
better due to the fact

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:28.740
that you can crank up the
learning rate a little bit.

01:13:28.740 --> 01:13:30.840
But if you do
soft-capping alone,

01:13:30.840 --> 01:13:32.600
you actually end up
losing performance.

01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:34.902
So there is a quality
degradation that happens.

01:13:34.902 --> 01:13:36.360
This is a very
strong intervention.

01:13:36.360 --> 01:13:39.020
You can never express
very confident signals

01:13:39.020 --> 01:13:41.700
in your softmax beyond
a certain point.

01:13:41.700 --> 01:13:43.930
So it does have some
negative consequences,

01:13:43.930 --> 01:13:46.790
but this is a very
safe way of stabilizing

01:13:46.790 --> 01:13:48.755
the outputs of your
attention-- or sorry,

01:13:48.755 --> 01:13:50.630
the inputs to your
attention, the logits that

01:13:50.630 --> 01:13:52.630
go into the softmax.

01:13:52.630 --> 01:13:56.630
So that's the end of the
stability components.

01:13:56.630 --> 01:13:59.190
I can pause for a moment here.

01:13:59.190 --> 01:14:02.850
And I'll talk about various
attention things after that.

01:14:06.470 --> 01:14:07.390
Cool.

01:14:07.390 --> 01:14:08.830
OK.

01:14:08.830 --> 01:14:09.330
All right.

01:14:09.330 --> 01:14:12.030
So the last thing I
want to talk about today

01:14:12.030 --> 01:14:15.670
is various interventions that
you can make to your attention.

01:14:15.670 --> 01:14:18.590
And as I was saying at the
beginning of this lecture,

01:14:18.590 --> 01:14:20.550
I'm only going to talk
about all the things

01:14:20.550 --> 01:14:23.970
that you can do to dance
all by all attention today.

01:14:23.970 --> 01:14:26.430
So if you were interested
in hearing about state space

01:14:26.430 --> 01:14:29.350
models or linear time
attention, sadly, today

01:14:29.350 --> 01:14:31.670
is not the day for you.

01:14:31.670 --> 01:14:33.450
The things that I do
want to talk about,

01:14:33.450 --> 01:14:36.670
which are really commonly
implemented attention

01:14:36.670 --> 01:14:40.210
interventions today, are
group query attention,

01:14:40.210 --> 01:14:42.890
which really saves inference
costs by reducing the number

01:14:42.890 --> 01:14:46.850
of heads and sparse or
sliding window attention,

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:51.030
which really originally came
from the GPT 3-ish family,

01:14:51.030 --> 01:14:55.690
but have now really been adopted
widely by most models that are

01:14:55.690 --> 01:14:59.410
looking to do long context,
unless they're doing exotic SSM

01:14:59.410 --> 01:15:01.810
stuff.

01:15:01.810 --> 01:15:06.450
So I'll start with group query
attention, or GQA, or MQA.

01:15:06.450 --> 01:15:10.510
I'm going to first set up the
need for these kinds of things.

01:15:10.510 --> 01:15:14.010
And then you'll hopefully
see what the trick is

01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:16.290
and why it's fairly natural.

01:15:16.290 --> 01:15:18.170
So for the moment,
we've been talking

01:15:18.170 --> 01:15:20.670
about training, and modeling,
and all these things,

01:15:20.670 --> 01:15:22.130
but let's take a pause.

01:15:22.130 --> 01:15:23.990
And now, let's think
about deployment.

01:15:23.990 --> 01:15:26.490
You've trained this
very big model.

01:15:26.490 --> 01:15:28.910
And now, you need to
serve it to lots of users.

01:15:28.910 --> 01:15:31.490
And you're going to
pay a cost for serving.

01:15:31.490 --> 01:15:34.310
And you're going to have
to, in abstract sense,

01:15:34.310 --> 01:15:36.050
pay for two different resources.

01:15:36.050 --> 01:15:39.220
You're going to have to pay
for your flops, the computation

01:15:39.220 --> 01:15:40.340
that you're performing.

01:15:40.340 --> 01:15:42.132
But you also have to
pay for another thing.

01:15:42.132 --> 01:15:43.920
You have to pay for
your memory accesses.

01:15:43.920 --> 01:15:46.180
Because the memory
accesses are also

01:15:46.180 --> 01:15:49.360
going to impact your systems
characteristics, your latency,

01:15:49.360 --> 01:15:50.280
your utilization.

01:15:50.280 --> 01:15:53.620
So you want both of
these things to be small.

01:15:53.620 --> 01:15:55.380
Now, let's think
about what happens

01:15:55.380 --> 01:15:57.660
during training
or alternatively,

01:15:57.660 --> 01:15:59.580
prefill, when you're
looking at your prompt,

01:15:59.580 --> 01:16:01.300
where someone gives
you the stuff.

01:16:01.300 --> 01:16:05.180
In this case, the total
arithmetic operations you have

01:16:05.180 --> 01:16:09.280
is order of magnitude,
batch size, sequence length,

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:10.240
hidden dim squared.

01:16:10.240 --> 01:16:12.562
That's roughly the size
of things that you get.

01:16:12.562 --> 01:16:14.520
And of course, we're
doing quadratic attention,

01:16:14.520 --> 01:16:17.660
so we've got d squared.

01:16:17.660 --> 01:16:20.180
We've got total memory accesses.

01:16:20.180 --> 01:16:22.740
What is our memory
access that we have here?

01:16:22.740 --> 01:16:25.700
We have batch times sequence
length times hidden dim

01:16:25.700 --> 01:16:28.860
plus the cost of
the softmax, which

01:16:28.860 --> 01:16:30.160
has an n squared component.

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:34.480
And then we've got a d squared
component for the projections.

01:16:34.480 --> 01:16:37.510
So the arithmetic intensity
here is pretty good.

01:16:37.510 --> 01:16:38.683
It's going to be 1 over k.

01:16:38.683 --> 01:16:39.850
This is the number of heads.

01:16:39.850 --> 01:16:41.925
So you need to have,
sorry, head dims.

01:16:41.925 --> 01:16:43.550
So your head dims
need to be big enough

01:16:43.550 --> 01:16:46.090
that you're multiplying some
reasonably sized matrices.

01:16:46.090 --> 01:16:48.750
And you've got a 1 over
bn, so your sequences

01:16:48.750 --> 01:16:50.550
need to be long enough
or your batch sizes

01:16:50.550 --> 01:16:51.870
need to be big enough.

01:16:51.870 --> 01:16:53.630
As long as both
of these are true,

01:16:53.630 --> 01:16:55.530
your GPUs are going
to be fully utilized.

01:16:55.530 --> 01:16:56.030
Great.

01:16:56.030 --> 01:16:59.590
You're using all
of your resources.

01:16:59.590 --> 01:17:02.430
Now, we have finished training.

01:17:02.430 --> 01:17:03.967
And now, we're
serving our users.

01:17:03.967 --> 01:17:05.050
How do we serve our users?

01:17:05.050 --> 01:17:07.670
We're going to generate
tokens and send it to them.

01:17:07.670 --> 01:17:11.230
Now, if we're doing that, I
can't parallelize the generation

01:17:11.230 --> 01:17:11.970
process.

01:17:11.970 --> 01:17:13.650
What I'm going to do is, I'm
going to generate a token.

01:17:13.650 --> 01:17:14.610
I'm going to condition on it.

01:17:14.610 --> 01:17:16.050
I'm going to generate
the next token.

01:17:16.050 --> 01:17:18.050
And I'm going to repeat
this process one by one.

01:17:18.050 --> 01:17:20.590
This is just the curse of
autoregressive language

01:17:20.590 --> 01:17:21.090
modeling.

01:17:21.090 --> 01:17:23.310
We have to do this.

01:17:23.310 --> 01:17:25.590
In order to do this, the
efficient way to do it

01:17:25.590 --> 01:17:29.550
is to maintain all of
the passkeys and queries

01:17:29.550 --> 01:17:32.050
that I've had in what's
called a KV cache.

01:17:32.050 --> 01:17:36.400
So the KV cache is going to
maintain this matrix of Q dot

01:17:36.400 --> 01:17:38.140
K over the past.

01:17:38.140 --> 01:17:40.340
And then whenever I need
to compute something new,

01:17:40.340 --> 01:17:45.100
I can reuse the submatrices that
I've already had from the past.

01:17:45.100 --> 01:17:46.880
And I only really
need to compute

01:17:46.880 --> 01:17:52.280
the new query key interactions
that I need to fill out

01:17:52.280 --> 01:17:54.100
the rest of this matrix.

01:17:54.100 --> 01:17:57.480
So every submatrix I've
computed before, I can keep.

01:17:57.480 --> 01:17:59.220
I only need to
compute my new one.

01:17:59.220 --> 01:18:00.680
So this saves a lot on compute.

01:18:00.680 --> 01:18:02.320
That's great.

01:18:02.320 --> 01:18:06.760
But the issue here is, now,
our arithmetic intensity

01:18:06.760 --> 01:18:07.860
is not so good.

01:18:07.860 --> 01:18:11.760
As you might intuit,
this KV cache approach

01:18:11.760 --> 01:18:16.480
is going to be reading
parameters all the time.

01:18:16.480 --> 01:18:18.560
Each time I have a
new step, I'm going

01:18:18.560 --> 01:18:19.985
to have to read
in my parameters.

01:18:19.985 --> 01:18:21.860
I'm going to have to
take these dot products.

01:18:21.860 --> 01:18:24.560
And I'm going to do
this once every step.

01:18:24.560 --> 01:18:26.020
And so now, what do I have?

01:18:26.020 --> 01:18:28.460
Well, you my total
memory-- oh, sorry.

01:18:28.460 --> 01:18:30.500
My total arithmetic
operations are the same.

01:18:30.500 --> 01:18:32.970
I'm multiplying the same
matrices still, just

01:18:32.970 --> 01:18:35.170
incrementally rather
than all at once.

01:18:35.170 --> 01:18:37.370
But because I'm doing
this incrementally,

01:18:37.370 --> 01:18:42.090
now, I have a memory
access pattern of batch

01:18:42.090 --> 01:18:46.090
by sequence, squared by
hidden dim plus sequence

01:18:46.090 --> 01:18:47.550
by hidden dim squared.

01:18:47.550 --> 01:18:50.450
And the second term
is not so pleasant.

01:18:50.450 --> 01:18:54.290
It used to be that it
was just d squared,

01:18:54.290 --> 01:18:56.968
but now, we've got
n times d squared.

01:18:56.968 --> 01:18:59.010
And if we compute the
arithmetic intensity, which

01:18:59.010 --> 01:19:04.130
is the ratio of these two guys,
now, we have n over d plus 1

01:19:04.130 --> 01:19:05.050
over b.

01:19:05.050 --> 01:19:08.330
So now, what we need is large
batches plus short sequence

01:19:08.330 --> 01:19:11.190
length or we need really
big model dimensions.

01:19:11.190 --> 01:19:14.110
So if we want to serve a
small model efficiently,

01:19:14.110 --> 01:19:17.610
this is not so good.

01:19:17.610 --> 01:19:19.630
This is really
difficult to deal with.

01:19:19.630 --> 01:19:22.390
This n over d term, this
first term over here,

01:19:22.390 --> 01:19:24.890
which is sequence
length over hidden dim,

01:19:24.890 --> 01:19:26.810
is very difficult to
reduce if we're doing

01:19:26.810 --> 01:19:28.850
this incremental computation.

01:19:28.850 --> 01:19:32.420
This is just a hard
thing to deal with.

01:19:32.420 --> 01:19:36.900
So this leads to this idea of
MQA, or Multi-Query Attention.

01:19:36.900 --> 01:19:38.660
Normally, you have
multiple heads

01:19:38.660 --> 01:19:40.493
in your attention operation.

01:19:40.493 --> 01:19:42.160
And you're going to
have different keys,

01:19:42.160 --> 01:19:44.160
different values, and
different queries.

01:19:44.160 --> 01:19:46.180
That's normally
how you do things.

01:19:46.180 --> 01:19:48.620
But one thing that
we could do is, maybe

01:19:48.620 --> 01:19:52.520
we can keep the Ks and the Vs
the same across all the heads.

01:19:52.520 --> 01:19:54.740
And the only thing that's
different across the heads

01:19:54.740 --> 01:19:56.020
are the queries.

01:19:56.020 --> 01:19:58.780
If we do this, then
this drastically

01:19:58.780 --> 01:20:01.900
removes the amount of
items that need to be

01:20:01.900 --> 01:20:03.040
moved in and out of memory.

01:20:03.040 --> 01:20:07.120
Because the KV cache are
now significantly smaller.

01:20:07.120 --> 01:20:10.180
These are all shared
across all the heads.

01:20:10.180 --> 01:20:13.540
This significantly reduces the
total memory access as well as

01:20:13.540 --> 01:20:15.040
the arithmetic intensity.

01:20:15.040 --> 01:20:18.400
And the key term that we
were talking about here,

01:20:18.400 --> 01:20:20.140
we had the n over d term.

01:20:20.140 --> 01:20:23.480
Now, we have h multiplying this.

01:20:23.480 --> 01:20:29.310
And so this h term allows us
to significantly reduce the--

01:20:29.310 --> 01:20:31.070
sorry, increase the
arithmetic intensity

01:20:31.070 --> 01:20:32.490
if we have a lot of heads.

01:20:32.490 --> 01:20:36.750
This is a significant gain
over what we had before.

01:20:36.750 --> 01:20:40.170
So this gets us significant
efficiency improvements,

01:20:40.170 --> 01:20:45.250
but the issue with MQA,
this is on the right here,

01:20:45.250 --> 01:20:48.850
you have one value and one
key for all these queries,

01:20:48.850 --> 01:20:51.430
you do, in fact, lose
significant expressive power

01:20:51.430 --> 01:20:53.150
if you do this.

01:20:53.150 --> 01:20:56.150
And so there's this trade off
between systems efficiency

01:20:56.150 --> 01:20:57.090
and expressiveness.

01:20:57.090 --> 01:20:59.990
And you might wonder,
is there a sweet spot

01:20:59.990 --> 01:21:02.590
in which we can
avoid trading off

01:21:02.590 --> 01:21:07.490
quite significantly expressive
power and computation?

01:21:07.490 --> 01:21:10.710
And that's where GQA, or Grouped
Query Attention, comes in.

01:21:10.710 --> 01:21:12.970
The original transformer
is multihead.

01:21:12.970 --> 01:21:15.350
We have queries and
keys for each head.

01:21:15.350 --> 01:21:18.730
In multi-query, we have
one key and value for each.

01:21:18.730 --> 01:21:20.890
For all the heads
in grouped query,

01:21:20.890 --> 01:21:23.178
we reduce the amount
of keys and values,

01:21:23.178 --> 01:21:24.970
but we keep the number
of queries the same.

01:21:24.970 --> 01:21:27.290
So we now have this ratio
that we can play with,

01:21:27.290 --> 01:21:30.000
which is the number key
heads or the number of value

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:33.160
heads while keeping the total
number of heads much larger

01:21:33.160 --> 01:21:33.660
than that.

01:21:33.660 --> 01:21:35.640
So this allows us to
very simply control

01:21:35.640 --> 01:21:39.000
the trade offs between
expressiveness and inference

01:21:39.000 --> 01:21:40.920
efficiency.

01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:44.200
There are other tricks from
DeepSeek V2, multihead latent

01:21:44.200 --> 01:21:48.880
attention that I'll
mention briefly next time,

01:21:48.880 --> 01:21:51.560
which have a different kind
of factorization structure

01:21:51.560 --> 01:21:53.240
and a different
set of trade offs.

01:21:53.240 --> 01:21:56.680
But really, the
nice thing about GQA

01:21:56.680 --> 01:21:59.380
is that, in practice, the
trade off is quite favorable.

01:21:59.380 --> 01:22:01.900
So if you have multihead,
your performance,

01:22:01.900 --> 01:22:06.000
this was in, I think, T5
days, if I remember right.

01:22:06.000 --> 01:22:08.820
This is your downstream
model performance.

01:22:08.820 --> 01:22:10.060
This is your time per sample.

01:22:10.060 --> 01:22:12.255
You want to reduce this
as much as possible.

01:22:12.255 --> 01:22:13.880
With multihead
attention, you have best

01:22:13.880 --> 01:22:16.360
performance but very high cost.

01:22:16.360 --> 01:22:22.240
With MQA, you have lower cost
but much lower performance.

01:22:22.240 --> 01:22:24.240
Similarly, if you make
your model smaller

01:22:24.240 --> 01:22:26.270
to try to hit your
performance targets,

01:22:26.270 --> 01:22:28.490
you get much worse
performance, GQA really

01:22:28.490 --> 01:22:30.030
does get the best
of both worlds.

01:22:30.030 --> 01:22:34.250
Very low inference cost,
nearly the same performance

01:22:34.250 --> 01:22:36.970
as your full multihead.

01:22:36.970 --> 01:22:40.370
And you see this
GQA group structure,

01:22:40.370 --> 01:22:43.890
where if you have a small
reduction in the number

01:22:43.890 --> 01:22:46.610
of heads, you
basically have most

01:22:46.610 --> 01:22:48.650
of the gains in your
performance, which

01:22:48.650 --> 01:22:51.530
allows you to keep most
of the expressive power

01:22:51.530 --> 01:22:54.390
while getting significant
inference improvements.

01:22:54.390 --> 01:22:57.370
And Percy will talk a bunch more
about the inference mechanics

01:22:57.370 --> 01:23:01.970
later, but this should give
you a flavor why models today,

01:23:01.970 --> 01:23:05.192
almost all, adopt this GQA
structure, because it gives you

01:23:05.192 --> 01:23:06.650
a lot of this
inference cost, which

01:23:06.650 --> 01:23:14.390
is really critical, without very
much of a expressiveness hit.

01:23:14.390 --> 01:23:14.890
Cool.

01:23:14.890 --> 01:23:17.390
Any questions for
GQA or KV cache?

01:23:17.390 --> 01:23:17.890
Yeah.

01:23:17.890 --> 01:23:24.780
[INAUDIBLE] given that you
have so many rules of thumbs

01:23:24.780 --> 01:23:27.460
for what hyperparameters are
[INAUDIBLE] to what extent are

01:23:27.460 --> 01:23:29.460
you still searching
over hyperparameters

01:23:29.460 --> 01:23:33.820
versus exploiting these
rules of thumb [INAUDIBLE]?

01:23:33.820 --> 01:23:35.240
I think it's a mix of both.

01:23:35.240 --> 01:23:38.020
I think every model
training run has some thesis

01:23:38.020 --> 01:23:40.060
about what can be varied.

01:23:40.060 --> 01:23:42.323
And so you see this in a
lot of the reports, where

01:23:42.323 --> 01:23:44.740
I think the hyperparameters
are often not where people are

01:23:44.740 --> 01:23:48.300
touching too much, but you
see architecture changes one

01:23:48.300 --> 01:23:50.360
at a time in a lot
of these reports.

01:23:50.360 --> 01:23:53.200
But it's very rare to go
and change everything up.

01:23:53.200 --> 01:23:55.620
I think Google is one
of the only orgs that

01:23:55.620 --> 01:23:58.700
seems to really spice things
up in a significant way.

01:23:58.700 --> 01:24:02.660
The Gemma series has done some
pretty interesting things.

01:24:02.660 --> 01:24:04.640
The most recent Gemma
4 release, for example,

01:24:04.640 --> 01:24:08.500
now has an individual embedding
for every layer in a way

01:24:08.500 --> 01:24:11.240
to control the trade offs
between memory use and flops.

01:24:11.240 --> 01:24:14.740
It's a very interesting set
of things that they've done.

01:24:14.740 --> 01:24:15.240
Oh, yeah.

01:24:15.240 --> 01:24:16.610
Back there.

01:24:16.610 --> 01:24:18.660
[INAUDIBLE] experiments
with dynamically

01:24:18.660 --> 01:24:22.520
altering some of these
parameters during training.

01:24:22.520 --> 01:24:23.720
During training.

01:24:23.720 --> 01:24:26.400
Let me think.

01:24:26.400 --> 01:24:27.840
Weight decay, yes.

01:24:27.840 --> 01:24:32.160
Weight decay, people change
in concert with learning rate.

01:24:32.160 --> 01:24:34.280
That is actually a
heuristic that people do.

01:24:34.280 --> 01:24:36.760
That works very well.

01:24:36.760 --> 01:24:39.200
Other than that, I
don't know if there's

01:24:39.200 --> 01:24:42.565
a lot of different hypers that
people change during training,

01:24:42.565 --> 01:24:44.440
especially because the
architecture ones just

01:24:44.440 --> 01:24:45.880
make training incompatible.

01:24:45.880 --> 01:24:49.800
So you can't really change
them while you're training.

01:24:49.800 --> 01:24:51.280
So I think weight
decay is probably

01:24:51.280 --> 01:24:52.447
the one that I can think of.

01:24:52.447 --> 01:24:54.840
The others are usually fixed.

01:24:54.840 --> 01:24:55.720
Yeah.

01:24:55.720 --> 01:25:01.040
So [INAUDIBLE] MQA, it's
not just inference time,

01:25:01.040 --> 01:25:02.358
it's a [INAUDIBLE].

01:25:02.358 --> 01:25:02.900
That's right.

01:25:02.900 --> 01:25:03.400
Yeah.

01:25:03.400 --> 01:25:07.840
You train with a certain
number of repeats.

01:25:07.840 --> 01:25:08.360
Cool.

01:25:08.360 --> 01:25:12.340
The last thing I'll talk about
is sliding window attention,

01:25:12.340 --> 01:25:14.280
which is a really old idea.

01:25:14.280 --> 01:25:18.220
GPT 3 used, actually, this--
if you read the paper,

01:25:18.220 --> 01:25:21.730
they'll say, we alternate
between full attention, where

01:25:21.730 --> 01:25:24.030
every position can attend
to everyone in the past,

01:25:24.030 --> 01:25:26.330
and a banded matrix
style attention,

01:25:26.330 --> 01:25:30.330
where you can attend to
everyone within a fixed window.

01:25:30.330 --> 01:25:34.090
And OpenAI has some early
work on these different kinds

01:25:34.090 --> 01:25:37.130
of attention patterns
that you can use.

01:25:37.130 --> 01:25:39.450
But actually, this has
become really, really popular

01:25:39.450 --> 01:25:41.570
over the past year.

01:25:41.570 --> 01:25:46.810
This idea of alternating the
big, full attention and a more

01:25:46.810 --> 01:25:49.490
local attention actually
hits a sweet spot

01:25:49.490 --> 01:25:52.930
for how to manage long
context performance while not

01:25:52.930 --> 01:25:57.090
paying too much for inference.

01:25:57.090 --> 01:26:01.110
I think the more recent
revival in open models,

01:26:01.110 --> 01:26:03.350
I would maybe say,
Cohere Command

01:26:03.350 --> 01:26:07.970
A was the first one I saw do it,
where they had this structure,

01:26:07.970 --> 01:26:11.010
where every four layers, they
would have a full attention that

01:26:11.010 --> 01:26:12.450
attended to everything.

01:26:12.450 --> 01:26:14.810
The three layers in between
would use a sliding window

01:26:14.810 --> 01:26:17.570
attention that would only be
able to look at local structure.

01:26:17.570 --> 01:26:20.980
And of course, as you go
up, sorry, in this case,

01:26:20.980 --> 01:26:23.740
down, because they ordered
the diagram the other way,

01:26:23.740 --> 01:26:26.460
as you go down
these blocks, you're

01:26:26.460 --> 01:26:28.523
aggregating local
information into global ones.

01:26:28.523 --> 01:26:29.940
So the local
attentions at the end

01:26:29.940 --> 01:26:32.520
can, of course, access
more global information,

01:26:32.520 --> 01:26:38.380
but this allows you to manage
the cost of having a really

01:26:38.380 --> 01:26:40.460
long context
without having to go

01:26:40.460 --> 01:26:42.780
for something like
a state space model

01:26:42.780 --> 01:26:44.200
or a more exotic intervention.

01:26:44.200 --> 01:26:46.580
And that's worked quite well.

01:26:46.580 --> 01:26:49.100
There's also some
innovation, where

01:26:49.100 --> 01:26:52.380
people change the embedding
format for the long range

01:26:52.380 --> 01:26:55.000
information, where they get
rid of things like RoPE.

01:26:55.000 --> 01:26:57.080
So you have no position
embeddings at all.

01:26:57.080 --> 01:26:58.455
So you're really
looking, almost,

01:26:58.455 --> 01:27:01.300
at bags, where the short
range information still

01:27:01.300 --> 01:27:02.880
gets position information.

01:27:02.880 --> 01:27:07.220
So people do all sorts kinds
of interventions involving

01:27:07.220 --> 01:27:10.580
these both embeddings
and alternating

01:27:10.580 --> 01:27:13.060
local and global structure.

01:27:13.060 --> 01:27:16.350
I'll say that this is
attention and in general,

01:27:16.350 --> 01:27:21.050
how to manage the trade
off between long context

01:27:21.050 --> 01:27:24.490
cost and performance is still
an active area of investigation.

01:27:24.490 --> 01:27:27.670
It's a place, where the most
architecture work and changes

01:27:27.670 --> 01:27:29.390
are still being done.

01:27:29.390 --> 01:27:32.670
We see, essentially, a bunch of
other models adopt this idea--

01:27:32.670 --> 01:27:35.790
Llama 4, most recently,
Gemma 4, OLMo 3,

01:27:35.790 --> 01:27:38.150
they all do this
combination of sliding

01:27:38.150 --> 01:27:40.670
window attention and full
attention, in their case,

01:27:40.670 --> 01:27:45.390
using full RoPE instead
of NoPE as the embedding.

01:27:45.390 --> 01:27:48.990
So as I said, this is becoming
really, really popular.

01:27:48.990 --> 01:27:50.763
Qwen3.5, which I've
put on the right,

01:27:50.763 --> 01:27:52.430
they're actually a
little bit different,

01:27:52.430 --> 01:27:56.790
because they alternate a state
space model called gated delta

01:27:56.790 --> 01:28:04.630
net and one full attention
for every four layers.

01:28:04.630 --> 01:28:06.530
So it's the same
alternating structure,

01:28:06.530 --> 01:28:08.568
but they're using a
different cheap layer.

01:28:08.568 --> 01:28:10.610
In their case, they're
using a state space model,

01:28:10.610 --> 01:28:13.270
I'll explain what that is
next lecture, instead of

01:28:13.270 --> 01:28:15.820
a sliding window
local attention.

01:28:15.820 --> 01:28:17.880
But you see, this is
like, I think, a new theme

01:28:17.880 --> 01:28:20.920
over the past year, where
open models are really

01:28:20.920 --> 01:28:23.380
trying to grapple with
long context performance.

01:28:23.380 --> 01:28:24.840
And the way to do
that, at least,

01:28:24.840 --> 01:28:28.120
so far, is to have these
hybrid models that aren't just

01:28:28.120 --> 01:28:30.380
global attention, aren't
just cheap attention,

01:28:30.380 --> 01:28:31.740
they're some of mix in between.

01:28:31.740 --> 01:28:34.080
And that seems to have
worked very well so

01:28:34.080 --> 01:28:37.760
far in a lot of these models.

01:28:37.760 --> 01:28:38.380
OK.

01:28:38.380 --> 01:28:39.080
Cool.

01:28:39.080 --> 01:28:41.360
So as I was trying
to emphasize, when

01:28:41.360 --> 01:28:43.302
you look across all
of these models,

01:28:43.302 --> 01:28:44.760
you start to see
a lot of patterns,

01:28:44.760 --> 01:28:47.280
and hopefully, a sense
of general understanding

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:51.120
about what things you can do and
what things are good defaults.

01:28:51.120 --> 01:28:54.240
We also see a lot of differences
in how we handle context

01:28:54.240 --> 01:28:56.640
and how we handle
position embeddings.

01:28:56.640 --> 01:28:58.745
Even tokenization,
there's some differences.

01:28:58.745 --> 01:29:00.620
So there are differences
across these models,

01:29:00.620 --> 01:29:02.800
but there's also commonalities
that hopefully, now, give you

01:29:02.800 --> 01:29:05.280
some intuition as you go
out, and do your assignments,

01:29:05.280 --> 01:29:08.200
and mess with the
leaderboard and so on.

01:29:08.200 --> 01:29:09.750
Thanks.

